WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.678 --> 00:00:14.682
[thumping]

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:18.143
[thumping intensifies]

00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:24.775
[thumping slows down]

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.987
[phone rings]

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.198
Hello, it's Yance.

00:00:31.281 --> 00:00:34.034
Hello, it's Demitri Jones
returning your phone call.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.369
-Hi, Miss Jones. How are you?
-OK.

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:41.124
I am not sure if you remember my name,
or my brother's name.

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:42.876
He was a homicide victim,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.089
back in 1992, when you were with the
Suffolk County District Attorney's Office.

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:49.132
-OK.
-His name was William Ford.

00:00:49.216 --> 00:00:52.135
You worked on the case
with Stephen O'Brien

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:54.262
and Detective James Hughes.

00:00:54.346 --> 00:00:56.014
OK, what do you want to know?

00:00:56.557 --> 00:00:58.517
I was calling to see if you were willing

00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:02.187
to, within, you know,
your legal restrictions,

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.023
answer some of the questions
that have been,

00:01:05.566 --> 00:01:08.443
sort of, plaguing me
for the last 22 years.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.822
No. I'm not going to do that.

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:14.867
OK. Do you mind if I ask you why?

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:19.622
Because as a prosecutor, everything that
happens in the Grand Jury is confidential.

00:01:19.705 --> 00:01:23.166
-So I'm not going to discuss it.
-Sure. Right. No.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.377
I'm asking about the investigation.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.173
Yeah, no, I'm not willing to discuss any
of my prior cases on film with anybody.

00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:33.135
-May I interview you by phone?
-No.

00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:36.429
-OK, and...
-I don't want to discuss the case with you.

00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:38.516
And you don't want to make any comment on your behalf?

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:40.559
I do not want to make any comment.

00:01:40.643 --> 00:01:42.978
-OK, Miss Jones. Thank you.
-Bye.

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:48.692
My son...

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.114
lying dead in a coffin,

00:01:55.908 --> 00:02:00.829
with the most peaceful look on his face.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:07.335
You, your sister...

00:02:12.675 --> 00:02:14.885
And I remember thinking...

00:02:25.771 --> 00:02:28.691
"How are we gonna make it without him?

00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:36.699
How will our life go on, without him?"

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.327
Even then,

00:02:43.455 --> 00:02:45.165
I was saying,

00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:49.211
"Wait until we get to court!

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:57.845
I said, "This is a young man who has never
been in trouble in his entire life.

00:02:59.054 --> 00:03:02.307
Wait until we get to court."

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.769
[Yance] So you were saying this
to yourself on the day of the funeral?

00:03:06.604 --> 00:03:08.647
"Wait until we get to court.

00:03:08.731 --> 00:03:11.775
This death is not going to be in vain."

00:03:12.568 --> 00:03:16.196
I'm not surprised
that the case didn't go to trial.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.867
I just want to know exactly all the reasons why.

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:21.702
[thumps]

00:03:21.785 --> 00:03:23.662
I'm not angry.

00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:34.339
I'm also not willing to accept

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:40.178
that someone else gets to say

00:03:40.721 --> 00:03:43.390
who William was.

00:03:44.808 --> 00:03:49.271
And if you're uncomfortable
with me asking these questions,

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.065
you should probably get up and go.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:05.328
[crickets chirping]

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.929
All of the years that we were growing up,

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:28.602
if we went through a section,
or passed a section

00:04:28.686 --> 00:04:31.146
that was predominantly white,

00:04:31.772 --> 00:04:32.815
you ran.

00:04:34.191 --> 00:04:38.904
That's when I started to realize
the economic difference.

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:44.201
OK, so these people weren't wealthy,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.663
but their wealth was that they were white.

00:04:49.915 --> 00:04:53.544
My father's name
was George Alexander Dunmore.

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:56.254
I was two when my father died.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.092
He had a severe asthma attack.

00:05:00.634 --> 00:05:04.221
He was taken to the hospital.

00:05:05.055 --> 00:05:08.559
There was a White waiting room,

00:05:08.642 --> 00:05:11.436
and a Colored waiting room,

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:15.398
and even though he was critically...

00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:19.027
in respiratory difficulty,

00:05:20.487 --> 00:05:22.114
he was made to wait.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:26.451
And during that period of time, he died.

00:05:29.663 --> 00:05:32.708
[Yance] Tell me the story of how
you met Dad. When did you meet Dad?

00:05:32.791 --> 00:05:35.127
[Barbara] Well, let's not say,
"When did I meet him?"

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.130
When did I become aware of him?

00:05:38.213 --> 00:05:39.882
[Yance] What's the difference?

00:05:40.423 --> 00:05:44.928
Because I saw him
when I was in the sixth grade,

00:05:45.012 --> 00:05:46.680
and he was in the seventh grade.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.644
I did not speak with him

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:57.858
until October of 1958.

00:05:58.526 --> 00:06:00.027
I was a sophomore,

00:06:00.861 --> 00:06:02.530
and he was a junior.

00:06:04.156 --> 00:06:07.826
And... where we lived,

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.287
in Charleston, South Carolina,

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.040
we always had a Coronation Ball

00:06:13.582 --> 00:06:15.458
at County Hall.

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:19.171
Your father came over.

00:06:19.254 --> 00:06:20.881
He asked me for a dance,

00:06:20.964 --> 00:06:24.552
and he asked me that night
if I would be his girl.

00:06:26.053 --> 00:06:29.306
I tried to be cool,

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:31.809
and let a few seconds pass

00:06:31.892 --> 00:06:34.311
before I said, "Yes!"

00:06:34.853 --> 00:06:37.355
I didn't say yes, I said,

00:06:37.898 --> 00:06:39.858
"Yeah, I guess so."

00:06:39.942 --> 00:06:43.737
You know?
But I was jumping for joy inside.

00:06:43.821 --> 00:06:46.532
Because I had loved this man from afar

00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:50.118
since I was in sixth grade.

00:06:53.205 --> 00:06:56.249
[gentle music]

00:06:59.211 --> 00:07:03.632
[Barbara] We got married July 10th, 1965.

00:07:05.008 --> 00:07:06.218
Now,

00:07:06.885 --> 00:07:10.931
I think that my husband...

00:07:11.014 --> 00:07:14.893
was a gorgeous man, OK?

00:07:15.352 --> 00:07:17.062
He was handsome.

00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:31.785
[laidback jazz]

00:07:37.207 --> 00:07:39.001
[Barbara] We moved to New York.

00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:46.466
I absolutely loved it.

00:07:49.261 --> 00:07:52.765
We found an apartment in Brooklyn.

00:07:57.352 --> 00:08:00.313
I enjoyed it immensely.

00:08:04.527 --> 00:08:07.613
It was the kind of apartment building

00:08:07.696 --> 00:08:12.159
that had a lot of old Jewish ladies
who lived there.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.829
And they would line up
in front of the window

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.832
in the afternoon, and they'd sit there.

00:08:20.668 --> 00:08:23.796
They were wonderful.

00:08:25.923 --> 00:08:28.175
I remember coming home one day,

00:08:28.258 --> 00:08:30.177
and one of them said,

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.597
"It looks as if
you're going to have a baby!"

00:08:33.681 --> 00:08:37.475
I said, "Oh, my goodness,
I thought I was hiding it."

00:08:38.727 --> 00:08:41.564
I was elated, you know.

00:08:41.647 --> 00:08:44.608
And when I sang he would get quiet,

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.862
and when I stopped,
he would move a little.

00:08:48.946 --> 00:08:51.574
And if I didn't start right back up,

00:08:51.657 --> 00:08:55.619
his little feet and hands would be moving.

00:08:59.623 --> 00:09:02.918
William was born in 1967.

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:10.425
Your father would say,
"That boy's gonna be so spoiled."

00:09:10.843 --> 00:09:13.679
He was. He was.

00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:18.141
[gentle music]

00:09:34.867 --> 00:09:37.703
[Barbara] I made an "A"
on the national teachers exam.

00:09:38.662 --> 00:09:41.414
I loved teaching.

00:09:41.957 --> 00:09:43.083
I loved it.

00:09:43.166 --> 00:09:47.921
Because I knew what being educated

00:09:48.463 --> 00:09:52.593
had done for my sisters and me.

00:09:54.595 --> 00:09:58.473
And I also knew the struggle.

00:09:58.557 --> 00:10:02.019
My mom stripped tobacco for a living,

00:10:02.645 --> 00:10:03.812
OK?

00:10:03.896 --> 00:10:07.733
She left school in the fourth grade,

00:10:08.651 --> 00:10:10.277
and went to work.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.780
I helped her learn how to read.

00:10:13.572 --> 00:10:15.448
I think she had no shame with me,

00:10:16.366 --> 00:10:18.285
because I was the youngest.

00:10:19.161 --> 00:10:20.996
And I was proud

00:10:21.539 --> 00:10:25.501
that I could help her read and write.

00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:33.551
[Yance] My mother started out her career
in New York City as an English teacher,

00:10:33.634 --> 00:10:37.971
and worked her way up to become
Principal of Thomas Jefferson High School

00:10:38.055 --> 00:10:39.723
in East New York.

00:10:39.807 --> 00:10:41.141
After 13 years there,

00:10:41.725 --> 00:10:46.188
she opened a school
for girls and women at Rikers,

00:10:46.271 --> 00:10:48.566
the jail that sits in...

00:10:49.191 --> 00:10:52.986
essentially at the end of the runway
at LaGuardia Airport.

00:10:56.406 --> 00:10:58.826
[ambient music]

00:10:58.909 --> 00:11:02.871
[Yance] She named it Rosewood,
and she developed programs

00:11:02.955 --> 00:11:06.709
that helped women leave prison
with skills,

00:11:06.792 --> 00:11:10.713
so that they would have options
when they got out of prison.

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:17.595
And my father just believed

00:11:17.678 --> 00:11:20.180
in my mother's ability to do anything.

00:11:22.516 --> 00:11:26.103
[Barbara] Your father worked
at Andrew Geller's.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.856
Fabulous shoes.

00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:34.069
Fantastic, beautiful shoes,
and I loved them.

00:11:38.532 --> 00:11:40.701
He wanted to be a draftsman.

00:11:44.622 --> 00:11:48.542
He took the test for the Transit Authority

00:11:48.626 --> 00:11:50.168
to become a motorman...

00:11:52.087 --> 00:11:54.297
but he could still go to school.

00:11:57.926 --> 00:11:59.344
Then you were born.

00:12:01.805 --> 00:12:03.932
When Lauren was born,

00:12:04.808 --> 00:12:06.476
he was overjoyed.

00:12:07.019 --> 00:12:10.480
He was overjoyed.

00:12:12.065 --> 00:12:15.569
But as you guys grew,
you were so rambunctious.

00:12:18.822 --> 00:12:22.242
We were looking for an apartment.

00:12:22.576 --> 00:12:25.579
We looked in Brooklyn.

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:31.168
Nothing that we found
did your father like.

00:12:32.127 --> 00:12:35.839
He did not like anything that we found.

00:12:35.923 --> 00:12:41.887
I must not have spoken to him
for a month, OK?

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.683
I would put pillows
in the middle of the bed,

00:12:46.725 --> 00:12:48.602
because it meant that

00:12:49.227 --> 00:12:51.271
we had to keep looking,

00:12:51.354 --> 00:12:55.693
and at the time, I did not know
how dead set he was

00:12:55.776 --> 00:12:57.695
on moving to Long Island.

00:13:01.239 --> 00:13:03.116
[Yance] My father drove the J Train,

00:13:04.034 --> 00:13:08.539
which ran on a loop through some of
the toughest neighborhoods in the city.

00:13:11.374 --> 00:13:15.629
From his motorman's cabin, he saw
a very different New York than my mother.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:20.342
He saw poverty, and crime,

00:13:21.301 --> 00:13:22.761
and violence,

00:13:24.137 --> 00:13:25.931
and a rapidly declining city.

00:13:28.684 --> 00:13:30.936
My father was not a fearful man.

00:13:31.812 --> 00:13:33.355
He was a realist.

00:13:36.191 --> 00:13:39.152
He had already gotten out
of the Jim Crow South in one piece,

00:13:39.236 --> 00:13:42.740
and he didn't want to put
his family or our future at risk.

00:13:43.574 --> 00:13:46.702
[Barbara] We came out one Saturday,

00:13:47.327 --> 00:13:49.538
and met Sam McCollough.

00:13:49.622 --> 00:13:52.875
Nice, you know. He lived in the community.

00:13:54.001 --> 00:13:57.546
He was the agent for the company

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.549
that bought this land
and built the houses.

00:14:01.133 --> 00:14:06.096
I was told later
that they wanted to attract...

00:14:07.139 --> 00:14:12.060
people who were employed
by the city, you know,

00:14:12.603 --> 00:14:16.273
whom they thought could afford the homes.

00:14:17.274 --> 00:14:21.444
A lot of... African Americans

00:14:21.529 --> 00:14:23.572
were moving out to Long Island.

00:14:24.197 --> 00:14:29.327
Civil servants, like bus drivers,
police officers, correction officers...

00:14:31.454 --> 00:14:36.126
and they would put them in pockets,
or neighborhoods in the different towns.

00:14:36.209 --> 00:14:37.836
You could go to certain towns...

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:42.174
go to Deer Park and find the same "new D,"
Amityville, find the same "new D,"

00:14:42.257 --> 00:14:46.929
but all the rest of Long Island
was, you know, predominantly white.

00:14:47.012 --> 00:14:50.473
So in that one neighborhood,

00:14:51.016 --> 00:14:52.309
it was a haven.

00:14:52.893 --> 00:14:55.062
When you would go out, you know...

00:14:56.855 --> 00:14:59.567
you might be running back for your life,
you know?

00:15:01.068 --> 00:15:03.612
Growing up
in a new development, a "new D"...

00:15:04.572 --> 00:15:06.156
was beautiful.

00:15:06.824 --> 00:15:08.366
It was wonderful.

00:15:08.992 --> 00:15:10.202
It was safe.

00:15:14.832 --> 00:15:19.336
[Barbara] I really,
really did not like it.

00:15:20.462 --> 00:15:22.005
Everybody was black.

00:15:22.089 --> 00:15:24.925
-[Yance] You think that was an accident?
-[Barbara] Of course not.

00:15:25.593 --> 00:15:26.468
But...

00:15:29.096 --> 00:15:30.639
I guess...

00:15:33.058 --> 00:15:35.477
not having grown up...

00:15:36.269 --> 00:15:42.150
in a home that belonged to us as a family,

00:15:43.485 --> 00:15:45.904
I wanted to ensure

00:15:45.988 --> 00:15:50.367
that you guys had it differently.

00:15:53.036 --> 00:15:55.288
This has been our home:

00:15:55.372 --> 00:15:59.417
147 Cone Avenue, Central Islip.

00:15:59.502 --> 00:16:02.755
[ice cream van playing tinkling music]

00:16:07.384 --> 00:16:10.262
[music continues]

00:16:15.934 --> 00:16:19.938
When they moved to the neighborhood
where I grew up in Central Islip,

00:16:20.689 --> 00:16:23.942
it was essentially moving back
into a segregated community.

00:16:25.819 --> 00:16:29.489
Segregation draws a line
around not just your neighborhood,

00:16:29.573 --> 00:16:30.908
but your life.

00:16:33.243 --> 00:16:37.455
"Sorry, you can't have more.
Sorry, you can't earn more.

00:16:37.998 --> 00:16:42.294
You can't shop here.
You can't live here. You can't move here.

00:16:42.377 --> 00:16:43.461
This is it for you."

00:16:46.965 --> 00:16:50.218
[Yance] While the houses were
affordably priced, it was deceptive.

00:16:51.011 --> 00:16:53.972
The taxes were high
and the public schools were bad.

00:16:55.599 --> 00:16:58.351
And now, on top of a mortgage
and car payments,

00:16:58.894 --> 00:17:01.730
they had to pay
a combined 36 years of tuition

00:17:01.814 --> 00:17:04.482
to put three kids through Catholic school.

00:17:11.406 --> 00:17:15.493
[Barbara] The bottom line was,
your father had to leave college.

00:17:15.578 --> 00:17:17.329
It got too much for him.

00:17:17.871 --> 00:17:20.541
He started working the night shift.

00:17:22.042 --> 00:17:26.463
Sometimes I'd be coming down
the Southern State, coming home,

00:17:26.547 --> 00:17:28.423
and he'd be going in.

00:17:28.507 --> 00:17:32.135
I would blow at him, he'd blow at me
and we would wave, you know.

00:17:32.219 --> 00:17:37.099
And for many, many, many years,
that's the way it was.

00:17:37.641 --> 00:17:40.310
I think it was good for you guys,

00:17:40.853 --> 00:17:45.023
but not good for a marriage to thrive.

00:17:47.442 --> 00:17:49.152
[Ray Charles: "Let the Good Times Roll"]

00:17:49.236 --> 00:17:53.156
Hey, everybody
Let's have some fun

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:57.244
You only live but once
And when you're dead, you're done

00:17:57.327 --> 00:17:59.788
So let the good times roll

00:18:01.498 --> 00:18:03.501
I said, let the good times roll

00:18:07.004 --> 00:18:09.172
I don't care if you're young or old

00:18:09.256 --> 00:18:12.259
You ought to get together
Let the good times roll

00:18:17.973 --> 00:18:21.769
[Yance] I loved that my mother,
especially,

00:18:22.435 --> 00:18:26.231
made it really clear that, no matter what,

00:18:27.274 --> 00:18:29.317
my brother, my sister and I,

00:18:29.401 --> 00:18:32.362
our principal job in life
was to love each other.

00:18:33.781 --> 00:18:35.407
I'm grateful for that directive.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.993
It's one of the reasons
why I miss my brother so much.

00:18:45.042 --> 00:18:48.295
William was seven years older than I was,

00:18:48.378 --> 00:18:50.798
and that is fantastic.

00:18:51.381 --> 00:18:54.635
Being nine years old
and having a 16-year-old brother

00:18:54.718 --> 00:18:57.137
who can get you into R-rated movies

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.890
and buy you comic books,

00:18:59.973 --> 00:19:02.893
and be able to drive you around...

00:19:02.976 --> 00:19:06.396
He would look at me and say,
"Kato, we're on a mission."

00:19:06.479 --> 00:19:08.356
No matter what we were doing.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.401
We could be going
to the grocery store to pick up...

00:19:12.528 --> 00:19:15.531
wax paper, you know,
and he was like, "We're on a mission."

00:19:15.614 --> 00:19:18.325
[male narrator] Another challenge
for the Green Hornet,

00:19:18.408 --> 00:19:22.037
his aide, Kato, and their rolling arsenal,
the Black Beauty.

00:19:22.788 --> 00:19:25.207
[Lauren] Kato was
the Green Hornet's sidekick.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:26.917
Kato was played by Bruce Lee.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:31.463
We grew up watching
old-school Batman and Robin,

00:19:31.547 --> 00:19:33.674
and The Green Hornet.

00:19:33.757 --> 00:19:36.218
Hornet gun. Let's roll, Kato.

00:19:36.927 --> 00:19:39.972
Being called Kato was like,
an honor, you know,

00:19:40.055 --> 00:19:41.682
because Bruce Lee was awesome.

00:19:41.765 --> 00:19:46.186
We both loved martial arts, you know,
and comic books and stuff like that.

00:19:46.269 --> 00:19:48.355
We'd, like, pass them around

00:19:48.438 --> 00:19:51.483
and say, "Look at this.
Do you see the artwork on this one?"

00:19:51.567 --> 00:19:54.194
"Oh, did you see the plotline on this one?
Oh, man!

00:19:54.277 --> 00:19:56.446
They're going to kill this guy off? No!"

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:57.740
You know?

00:19:58.657 --> 00:20:00.576
It was just awesome.

00:20:03.621 --> 00:20:06.456
[Kevin] Football is how we met.

00:20:07.833 --> 00:20:11.879
His dad and my dad,
and his self and myself,

00:20:11.962 --> 00:20:13.922
were all on the same team.

00:20:14.006 --> 00:20:16.759
And that's how we began our friendship.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:20.596
Ford was a center, I was a defensive end,

00:20:21.138 --> 00:20:25.392
and we used to rely on each other
for rides back and forth to practice.

00:20:25.475 --> 00:20:26.309
[Kevin laughs]

00:20:26.393 --> 00:20:30.105
From that time... until he passed away,

00:20:30.188 --> 00:20:32.983
I would pretty much say
we were pretty much inseparable.

00:20:35.528 --> 00:20:36.529
Me and William.

00:20:38.531 --> 00:20:40.824
[Kevin] Ford was protective
of those around him,

00:20:40.908 --> 00:20:42.701
especially his sisters.

00:20:43.994 --> 00:20:46.288
[Lauren] I was 15.

00:20:46.371 --> 00:20:50.208
He was the first person to tell me
I looked pretty, you know?

00:20:50.292 --> 00:20:52.335
He's like, "Now look at yourself."

00:20:54.171 --> 00:20:56.590
And he's like, "You're pretty."

00:20:59.092 --> 00:21:02.763
And I'm like, "No, I'm not."
And he's like, "Yes, yes, you are."

00:21:06.141 --> 00:21:08.018
That's just something I never forgot.

00:21:14.608 --> 00:21:18.486
[Yance] My parents wanted
to raise remarkable kids.

00:21:19.279 --> 00:21:22.783
Our blackness, and what it meant
to be black in America,

00:21:22.866 --> 00:21:25.077
and how to survive being black in America,

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:28.581
and the resilience that needed
to be black in America,

00:21:28.664 --> 00:21:30.332
as well as the pride,

00:21:30.415 --> 00:21:33.836
was something that our parents
instilled in us extraordinarily well.

00:21:34.503 --> 00:21:38.381
But it didn't occur to them
that there were other things

00:21:38.465 --> 00:21:40.467
that their kids might struggle with.

00:21:43.011 --> 00:21:46.389
William's room had been
directly across the hall from my parents.

00:21:46.473 --> 00:21:49.184
He quickly...

00:21:52.104 --> 00:21:53.021
[sighs]

00:21:53.105 --> 00:21:55.232
OK, what I remember...

00:22:03.406 --> 00:22:07.620
There are five steps
from the main level of the house

00:22:07.703 --> 00:22:09.329
down into the basement.

00:22:10.664 --> 00:22:13.751
By that summer,
William had moved to the basement.

00:22:13.834 --> 00:22:15.878
I would sneak into William's room

00:22:15.961 --> 00:22:19.507
and I'd read his magazines, and...

00:22:20.549 --> 00:22:22.885
You know, I read a lot of Playboy...

00:22:24.219 --> 00:22:25.345
that summer!

00:22:26.138 --> 00:22:30.267
I read a lot of Forum magazine
that summer.

00:22:34.522 --> 00:22:35.856
And...

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:40.694
that was something that I...

00:22:41.945 --> 00:22:43.363
felt, really...

00:22:44.364 --> 00:22:45.741
both...

00:22:47.993 --> 00:22:50.996
excited by, and ashamed of.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.877
Because I knew I was queer,

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:00.005
and there was no one
to talk about it with.

00:23:00.088 --> 00:23:05.844
[all sing] Happy birthday, dear Yance!

00:23:05.928 --> 00:23:10.182
Happy birthday to you!

00:23:10.265 --> 00:23:12.685
[cheering, applause]

00:23:14.687 --> 00:23:19.858
[The Crests: "Sixteen Candles" playing]

00:23:22.653 --> 00:23:25.739
[Yance] I never asked...
I never asked my parents questions.

00:23:28.283 --> 00:23:30.703
You know, we had health class in school,

00:23:30.786 --> 00:23:34.540
so I learned about sex and childbirth...

00:23:35.541 --> 00:23:37.209
from Catholic nuns.

00:23:38.919 --> 00:23:42.548
Instead of going to math class,
I went to the library and found...

00:23:42.631 --> 00:23:44.466
you know, Rita Mae Brown books,

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:47.595
and even though they were corny,
I read those.

00:23:48.554 --> 00:23:51.515
That's how I learned that there were
other gay people in the world.

00:23:55.728 --> 00:23:58.480
I never told William that I was queer,

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:01.942
and I wish I had.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:12.452
That's right, the basement room
was my brother's last room,

00:24:12.536 --> 00:24:14.371
and he was living at home,

00:24:15.831 --> 00:24:17.165
when he was killed.

00:24:22.838 --> 00:24:24.464
[Lauren] I think it was a weekend,

00:24:24.548 --> 00:24:28.426
because William and Lesline
had taken me bowling.

00:24:29.469 --> 00:24:31.013
It was a fun night.

00:24:31.096 --> 00:24:33.599
I bowled a 96.

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:36.685
On the way back,

00:24:37.477 --> 00:24:39.104
I was sitting behind William.

00:24:39.187 --> 00:24:40.856
Lesline was driving.

00:24:41.481 --> 00:24:45.736
We were going to take the shortcut
onto Brightside, and as we were turning,

00:24:45.819 --> 00:24:50.115
I saw this tow truck
that didn't have any lights on,

00:24:51.199 --> 00:24:54.620
and before I could say, "Look out,"

00:24:55.328 --> 00:24:57.748
bam, we were in a car accident.

00:25:01.334 --> 00:25:03.378
[Lauren] William helped me out of the car.

00:25:03.461 --> 00:25:05.047
He asked me if I was OK.

00:25:06.131 --> 00:25:07.633
I said yes,

00:25:07.716 --> 00:25:10.678
and then he walked me
over to the tree stump,

00:25:11.469 --> 00:25:14.014
sat me down, said, "Stay here."

00:25:14.598 --> 00:25:17.643
The tow truck belonged
to some kind of body shop,

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:22.606
and they said that
if they didn't file a police report,

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:24.858
that they would fix Lesline's car.

00:25:25.776 --> 00:25:29.196
[Lauren] All I knew was that it was
being fixed by the guys who hit it.

00:25:33.534 --> 00:25:35.703
[Kevin] Once I found out
where the car was at,

00:25:35.786 --> 00:25:38.496
I said to Ford that,

00:25:38.581 --> 00:25:43.293
"I don't think the place is legit.
I don't think it is what you think it is."

00:25:45.212 --> 00:25:46.964
That, "You got to be very careful."

00:25:47.047 --> 00:25:50.634
That they weren't the kind of people
you wanted to play around with.

00:25:52.553 --> 00:25:54.888
[Barbara] I was on Rikers Island
at the time,

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:58.976
and Lesline got a job working for me.

00:25:59.518 --> 00:26:01.979
One day, we were driving home,

00:26:02.062 --> 00:26:04.272
and she asked me if I would stop by

00:26:04.356 --> 00:26:07.693
so she could see if her car was finished.

00:26:07.776 --> 00:26:11.154
But it wasn't finished.

00:26:12.072 --> 00:26:14.783
And that's when the drama started.

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.293
[Kevin] Sitting downstairs on the sofa,

00:26:25.377 --> 00:26:28.213
and the phone rang, and it was Ford,
and he says,

00:26:28.296 --> 00:26:30.549
"Hey, what's going on? I need a favor."

00:26:30.633 --> 00:26:35.012
So I said, "What's up?" And he goes,
"I need to go to pick up the car."

00:26:35.554 --> 00:26:37.389
It was late, I thought it was weird,

00:26:37.472 --> 00:26:41.351
but at that point I'd known the shop
to be open all types of hours of night.

00:26:41.434 --> 00:26:45.773
So I said, "Oh, great! Are you sure
you're picking up the car?"

00:26:45.856 --> 00:26:48.859
He's like, "Yeah, it's all done,"
and I'm like, "This is over.

00:26:48.942 --> 00:26:50.528
Let's get in the car. It's over."

00:26:50.611 --> 00:26:54.615
I got in the car, went the three blocks
to get to Ford's house.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:59.620
He came outside, seemed in a good mood,
so I didn't think anything.

00:26:59.703 --> 00:27:01.079
He gets in the car.

00:27:01.747 --> 00:27:04.625
I remember turning the car on and saying,

00:27:04.708 --> 00:27:06.209
"Let's go to Queens".

00:27:06.293 --> 00:27:08.837
"Queens" was our strip club's...

00:27:09.421 --> 00:27:10.923
code for going to a strip club.

00:27:11.006 --> 00:27:12.883
"Let's go to Queens, let's get a beer". Go hangout!

00:27:12.966 --> 00:27:15.553
He goes, "Yeah, sure.
Let me just go and get the car."

00:27:15.636 --> 00:27:17.012
I said OK.

00:27:17.095 --> 00:27:20.182
We got up the street
to the first stop sign,

00:27:20.265 --> 00:27:23.727
and again I said,
"I think we should go get a beer,"

00:27:23.811 --> 00:27:27.480
and he says, "Kev, you're bugging.
Take me to get the car."

00:27:27.565 --> 00:27:31.109
We turn, we start heading down Ferndale
towards Brightside, and...

00:27:31.193 --> 00:27:33.737
once you make that turn
it's pretty much right there.

00:27:36.156 --> 00:27:39.618
I remember him going into the yard
and somebody coming out of the shop door

00:27:39.702 --> 00:27:41.119
and meeting him in the yard.

00:27:41.203 --> 00:27:45.332
And immediately words started getting
exchanged. And I said, "Oh, here we go."

00:27:45.415 --> 00:27:49.211
And it's nothing physical,
and it's nothing too out of control,

00:27:50.378 --> 00:27:52.798
so I'm just, like, not saying nothing.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:56.134
I'm just kind of standing there,
and then...

00:27:57.135 --> 00:28:00.598
someone walked out of the garage area.

00:28:03.809 --> 00:28:06.311
Three feet, four feet,
stopped dead in his tracks.

00:28:06.394 --> 00:28:08.355
Ford turned and said,

00:28:08.438 --> 00:28:11.316
"Kevin, that's the kid
that cursed out my mother."

00:28:12.568 --> 00:28:17.072
And then, back we grew up,
nobody disrespects your mom.

00:28:17.155 --> 00:28:20.743
At that point, I knew somebody
was going to get into a fight.

00:28:21.284 --> 00:28:24.913
There was nothing for me to say,
so I just said, "OK."

00:28:25.497 --> 00:28:28.416
And Ford went to walk towards him.

00:28:28.501 --> 00:28:30.460
The kid turned,

00:28:31.378 --> 00:28:33.296
went back in the garage and made a left.

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:34.715
Disappeared.

00:28:34.798 --> 00:28:37.718
The minute Ford walked
into that garage door and made a left,

00:28:37.801 --> 00:28:39.344
I heard a pop.

00:28:40.053 --> 00:28:43.056
And I said to the guy, I said,
"What the hell was that?"

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.933
And he says, "I don't know."

00:28:45.017 --> 00:28:47.978
I said, "It sounded
like an air compressor,"

00:28:48.061 --> 00:28:50.230
and then I said,
"Do you guys have a gun here?"

00:28:50.313 --> 00:28:52.190
He said,
"Yeah, we have one in the back."

00:28:52.775 --> 00:28:54.276
I said, "Oh, shit."

00:28:57.029 --> 00:28:58.531
[Barbara] The phone rang.

00:29:01.158 --> 00:29:03.493
Kevin was on the phone.

00:29:04.703 --> 00:29:06.079
"Ms. Ford,

00:29:07.289 --> 00:29:10.292
you need to come to Super Stang."

00:29:12.961 --> 00:29:14.838
I said, "Why?"

00:29:14.922 --> 00:29:17.132
He said, "Something happened."

00:29:17.758 --> 00:29:19.760
She takes the car.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:25.891
And all I know is that
she pulled out of the driveway,

00:29:25.974 --> 00:29:28.936
heading down Cone Avenue.

00:29:38.737 --> 00:29:40.698
[Barbara] There was a barricade.

00:29:44.743 --> 00:29:47.913
No officer spoke to me.

00:29:50.498 --> 00:29:52.459
No officer...

00:29:53.919 --> 00:29:55.588
would look at me.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.466
Kevin came to me,

00:30:00.968 --> 00:30:03.386
and he said,

00:30:04.597 --> 00:30:07.558
"Mark Reilly shot William."

00:30:09.101 --> 00:30:12.062
And I said to the officer,

00:30:12.771 --> 00:30:14.106
you know...

00:30:15.566 --> 00:30:19.486
"Where is my son?
I want to be with my son."

00:30:22.239 --> 00:30:25.200
And he told me that...

00:30:28.036 --> 00:30:31.790
they had taken him to LIPA,

00:30:33.083 --> 00:30:35.168
to airlift him...

00:30:36.837 --> 00:30:38.296
to Stony Brook.

00:30:40.132 --> 00:30:42.551
We went to the emergency room.

00:30:43.594 --> 00:30:45.929
The area where he was,

00:30:46.555 --> 00:30:47.389
was...

00:30:48.557 --> 00:30:50.726
from here to that wall...

00:30:54.855 --> 00:30:58.734
with beds, you know,
where people were being treated.

00:31:07.450 --> 00:31:08.619
And...

00:31:14.875 --> 00:31:16.835
he was laying there.

00:31:22.049 --> 00:31:24.467
And he looked so peaceful.

00:31:30.348 --> 00:31:33.727
[Yance, reading] "The body is that
of a well-developed obese black male.

00:31:34.269 --> 00:31:36.939
The body weighs 240 pounds,

00:31:37.022 --> 00:31:39.107
is five feet, eight inches in height,

00:31:39.191 --> 00:31:42.861
and appears compatible
with the reported age of 24 years.

00:31:43.696 --> 00:31:46.782
Cause of death: gunshot wound of chest.

00:31:46.865 --> 00:31:49.242
Manner of death: homicide."

00:31:52.705 --> 00:31:54.540
[Lauren] Eleven o'clock rolls by.

00:31:54.998 --> 00:31:59.419
Midnight comes by,
and I haven't heard anything from Mom.

00:32:00.212 --> 00:32:02.965
And Dad gets home.
It's about one o'clock in the morning.

00:32:03.048 --> 00:32:06.927
"What are you doing up?
Where is your mother? Where is William?"

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:09.805
And I'm like, "He was in a fight
and Mom went to get him."

00:32:11.014 --> 00:32:13.100
I hear a car pull up outside.

00:32:14.727 --> 00:32:18.355
You know, Dad's looking at her,
I'm downstairs looking at her,

00:32:19.189 --> 00:32:22.192
and she comes in and she's like,
"He's gone."

00:32:24.778 --> 00:32:26.864
-[Yance] Is that how she said it?
-Yeah.

00:32:33.411 --> 00:32:35.706
Dad just held her,

00:32:37.708 --> 00:32:39.627
and held me, and...

00:32:41.504 --> 00:32:42.630
cried.

00:32:42.713 --> 00:32:44.381
We all just cried.

00:32:46.967 --> 00:32:48.176
You know?

00:32:50.804 --> 00:32:52.806
Dad sent me upstairs to my room.

00:32:54.767 --> 00:32:57.227
Whatever she explained,
she explained to Dad.

00:32:57.770 --> 00:32:59.563
She never explained it to me.

00:33:01.815 --> 00:33:04.902
[soft, sinister music]

00:33:07.696 --> 00:33:09.865
[Yance, reading]
"My boss asked me to call home.

00:33:10.616 --> 00:33:13.619
She was the Dean
of Multicultural Recruitment at Hamilton.

00:33:14.662 --> 00:33:16.329
I asked her what was wrong.

00:33:17.414 --> 00:33:18.957
She told me to stay calm.

00:33:19.792 --> 00:33:22.085
I told her not to tell me to stay calm.

00:33:23.921 --> 00:33:25.548
My dad answered the phone.

00:33:25.631 --> 00:33:27.382
I said, 'Is it Mom?'

00:33:28.551 --> 00:33:30.468
He said, 'Your mother is fine.'

00:33:31.136 --> 00:33:34.347
I said, 'Lauren?'
Or maybe I said, 'What's happening?'

00:33:35.348 --> 00:33:37.475
He said, 'Your brother is gone.'

00:33:40.103 --> 00:33:42.565
The next part of the conversation
is a blur to me.

00:33:43.231 --> 00:33:45.275
I remember screaming
and punching the wall.

00:33:46.569 --> 00:33:48.278
Packing a few clothes,

00:33:48.361 --> 00:33:50.447
including the one dress I owned,

00:33:50.531 --> 00:33:51.574
and leaving.

00:33:54.242 --> 00:33:56.078
That was the beginning.

00:33:59.164 --> 00:34:00.749
I remember walking into the house

00:34:00.833 --> 00:34:03.794
and feeling immediately
like I was surrounded by strangers,

00:34:04.336 --> 00:34:07.631
even though they were
people I had known for most of my life.

00:34:08.674 --> 00:34:13.428
I remember walking in
and having to suppress the urge

00:34:14.012 --> 00:34:16.682
to tell everyone
to get the fuck out of the house.

00:34:18.767 --> 00:34:23.105
My mother was on the couch,
sleeping and crying.

00:34:24.439 --> 00:34:25.983
My father was...

00:34:28.861 --> 00:34:29.862
stunned.

00:34:31.113 --> 00:34:32.573
Imploding.

00:34:34.282 --> 00:34:35.659
My sister...

00:34:36.535 --> 00:34:37.870
was alone.

00:34:43.501 --> 00:34:49.006
And I felt like all the people
who were there were in the way,

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:53.552
and were obstructing my ability to see
where the next threat was coming from.

00:34:58.724 --> 00:35:01.101
If I could have turned around and left,

00:35:03.604 --> 00:35:06.439
I would have, but that wasn't an option."

00:35:07.733 --> 00:35:10.736
[soft, sinister music]

00:35:25.208 --> 00:35:28.879
[Barbara] We went to meet with the DA.

00:35:29.421 --> 00:35:33.676
I'm not going to lie to you and say,

00:35:33.759 --> 00:35:37.470
"Well, the DA said this
and the detective said that."

00:35:38.221 --> 00:35:40.015
This is what I can tell you:

00:35:40.098 --> 00:35:42.017
from what they said,

00:35:42.851 --> 00:35:47.022
I did not feel that we were received

00:35:47.105 --> 00:35:51.193
as parents of a victim.

00:35:53.028 --> 00:35:56.782
OK? We weren't received
as parents of a victim.

00:35:57.324 --> 00:35:59.910
We were received as...

00:36:01.704 --> 00:36:07.668
folks being informed
that an investigation had to be conducted,

00:36:08.335 --> 00:36:10.212
and would be conducted.

00:36:10.295 --> 00:36:11.421
OK?

00:36:16.343 --> 00:36:17.595
I...

00:36:18.929 --> 00:36:22.265
was foolish enough to think

00:36:22.891 --> 00:36:25.561
that, well, you know,

00:36:25.644 --> 00:36:27.020
"It's gonna be OK."

00:36:28.438 --> 00:36:30.649
And then one day
I got a call to come...

00:36:32.025 --> 00:36:33.276
see Ms. Jones.

00:36:33.819 --> 00:36:36.321
We had no experience
with this kind of stuff.

00:36:36.404 --> 00:36:39.700
Maybe I should have had a lawyer.
I don't know what should have went on.

00:36:39.783 --> 00:36:41.535
She called and said "Meet me."

00:36:41.619 --> 00:36:45.873
You just go there, answer questions...
you know, about that night.

00:36:45.956 --> 00:36:47.040
That's what I thought.

00:36:47.124 --> 00:36:51.253
To be honest, I didn't know she was a DA.
I was thinking I was going to a detective.

00:36:51.336 --> 00:36:54.632
I was shocked
when she walked in the room.

00:36:55.508 --> 00:36:57.801
She really started
with the stuff with the gym.

00:36:57.885 --> 00:37:01.138
She was like, "You look
like you're in pretty good shape,"

00:37:01.221 --> 00:37:03.516
and, "How much do you weigh?"

00:37:04.057 --> 00:37:07.227
She asked something about William's size,

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:11.148
kind of, more telling me than asking me,
and I don't think she sat down.

00:37:11.231 --> 00:37:14.276
She kept walking back and forth
in front of the table.

00:37:14.359 --> 00:37:15.528
It was just...

00:37:15.611 --> 00:37:16.695
I was like,

00:37:16.779 --> 00:37:21.659
"Why are they asking me all these
questions about strength and gym and..."

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:25.037
It was not a lot about that night,

00:37:25.579 --> 00:37:26.997
or about...

00:37:29.124 --> 00:37:30.167
William.

00:37:33.086 --> 00:37:35.839
[Barbara] What they spent time on

00:37:36.549 --> 00:37:42.054
was investigating his background.

00:37:44.056 --> 00:37:46.058
That's what they spent time on.

00:37:48.686 --> 00:37:52.230
[Yance] Day by day, you hear
that your son is being investigated.

00:37:53.023 --> 00:37:54.775
Day by day, you hear rumors.

00:37:58.696 --> 00:38:00.739
And you grow more and more afraid.

00:38:01.824 --> 00:38:05.703
[soft, sinister music]

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:12.292
[phone rings]

00:38:16.296 --> 00:38:18.882
[ringing continues]

00:38:21.051 --> 00:38:23.512
Countless number of times,

00:38:24.888 --> 00:38:26.849
at all hours of the night,

00:38:28.767 --> 00:38:31.228
during the summer
after my brother was killed,

00:38:31.812 --> 00:38:33.897
I could look outside the window,

00:38:33.981 --> 00:38:36.233
and there was a car
parked across the street.

00:38:37.234 --> 00:38:40.195
That car, and whoever was in that car

00:38:40.738 --> 00:38:44.533
was watching our house
and trying to intimidate my parents.

00:38:46.409 --> 00:38:49.329
The phone rang in the middle of the night,

00:38:49.412 --> 00:38:51.414
every night for months.

00:38:52.875 --> 00:38:57.420
When I was home, I unplugged
all of the phones in the house

00:38:57.505 --> 00:38:59.047
except for the one in my room,

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:01.717
so my parents could
sleep through the night.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:05.721
So that they wouldn't have
to pick up the phone and say, "Hello?"

00:39:07.055 --> 00:39:09.683
and not have anyone respond.

00:39:10.225 --> 00:39:15.105
So they wouldn't have to hang up the phone
and go to the window,

00:39:15.188 --> 00:39:18.108
and see the car sitting across the street.

00:39:21.194 --> 00:39:23.572
[Yance] Having grown up in the South,

00:39:23.656 --> 00:39:27.701
where the cops and the Klan
were one and the same,

00:39:27.785 --> 00:39:32.665
my parents didn't turn
to the police for protection.

00:39:33.624 --> 00:39:37.127
They had already felt that the police
had turned their own son

00:39:37.210 --> 00:39:39.755
into the prime suspect in his own murder.

00:39:42.090 --> 00:39:45.636
So not only is the phone ringing,
not only is there a car across the street,

00:39:45.719 --> 00:39:48.055
but there's the growing sense

00:39:48.681 --> 00:39:52.976
that the DA is going
to actually let this kid

00:39:53.060 --> 00:39:55.312
get away with murder.

00:40:00.568 --> 00:40:05.447
"May 20th, 1992.
District Attorney James Catterson.

00:40:06.323 --> 00:40:10.994
Dear Mr. Catterson,
I am the mother of William Ford Jr.

00:40:11.078 --> 00:40:14.206
a 24-year-old man
who was murdered by Mark Reilly.

00:40:15.708 --> 00:40:19.044
My family and I have been working
with Detective James Hughes

00:40:19.127 --> 00:40:21.254
and Assistant DA Stephen O'Brien

00:40:21.338 --> 00:40:23.465
regarding the investigation of this crime.

00:40:24.508 --> 00:40:28.596
We have waited and worked cooperatively
with the investigation.

00:40:29.221 --> 00:40:31.765
There is, however,
a nagging doubt which I have,

00:40:31.849 --> 00:40:34.059
regarding the prosecution of this crime.

00:40:35.435 --> 00:40:39.732
Now, nearly two months later
after his deliberate death,

00:40:39.815 --> 00:40:44.277
I and my family have yet to receive
even a note from the Reillys.

00:40:45.278 --> 00:40:47.114
Adding insult to injury,

00:40:47.197 --> 00:40:51.409
I now fear that your office has not yet
fully embraced the advocacy

00:40:51.493 --> 00:40:54.872
for the people of the State of New York
in the prosecution of this case.

00:40:55.748 --> 00:40:56.665
Why?

00:40:57.457 --> 00:41:02.880
It is because we are being told, quote,
'It is entirely up to the Grand Jury.'

00:41:04.089 --> 00:41:07.300
I believe that
the strength of the presentation

00:41:07.384 --> 00:41:11.639
made to the Grand Jury by your office
is the determining factor.

00:41:13.015 --> 00:41:17.102
My son was not armed,
not violent, not aggressing.

00:41:17.185 --> 00:41:20.606
In no way is his death justifiable.

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:25.986
I intend never to rest
until his murderer is brought to justice.

00:41:27.154 --> 00:41:31.033
With the advocacy of your office
for the people of the State of New York,

00:41:31.116 --> 00:41:33.243
I trust that it will be soon,

00:41:33.326 --> 00:41:36.079
for we too, are the people.

00:41:37.039 --> 00:41:38.498
Barbara Dunmore-Ford."

00:41:42.711 --> 00:41:45.923
You know, the Grand Jury
is sort of this mystery, right, to people,

00:41:46.006 --> 00:41:48.300
And it's written
right into our Constitution.

00:41:48.383 --> 00:41:50.969
So really, it shouldn't be a mystery,
but it is.

00:41:51.053 --> 00:41:54.973
You know, the Bill of Rights calls
for a Grand Jury in felony cases,

00:41:55.057 --> 00:42:00.437
and that allows the prosecutor to present
its evidence to a neutral party,

00:42:00.521 --> 00:42:04.066
whether it's a Grand Jury
or a judge in a preliminary hearing,

00:42:04.149 --> 00:42:05.693
to show that there's two things:

00:42:05.776 --> 00:42:09.154
one, that there is probable cause
that a crime has been committed,

00:42:09.237 --> 00:42:13.075
and probable cause that this is
the individual who committed the crime.

00:42:19.164 --> 00:42:24.252
So at a trial, we have to prove beyond
reasonable doubt, to a moral certainty,

00:42:24.336 --> 00:42:27.673
that the person charged is
the defendant who committed the crime.

00:42:27.756 --> 00:42:30.342
But in the Grand Jury
it's just probable cause.

00:42:35.848 --> 00:42:39.977
In order for the case to go to a trial,
the Grand Jury would have had to...

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:43.731
I mean, I wasn't there, but presumably
they would have voted a True Bill,

00:42:43.814 --> 00:42:46.358
saying that there was
probable cause to move forward.

00:42:54.491 --> 00:42:58.161
So a Grand Jury is somewhat mysterious,
I think, to the general public.

00:42:58.245 --> 00:43:01.206
Another reason that is,
is that it's secret.

00:43:02.165 --> 00:43:04.334
And, you know, that actually...

00:43:04.417 --> 00:43:08.046
I think some people
get concerned with that.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:11.049
It's actually supposed to be
a protection for the defendant.

00:43:11.133 --> 00:43:14.011
And the idea there is, you know,

00:43:14.094 --> 00:43:17.681
if the Grand Jury decides
that there is not probable cause,

00:43:17.765 --> 00:43:21.018
that the person should not have
the stigma of, you know,

00:43:21.101 --> 00:43:23.854
having been brought before a Grand Jury.

00:43:24.396 --> 00:43:25.731
And, you know,

00:43:25.814 --> 00:43:30.027
the disclosure of secret Grand Jury
material can be a crime in jurisdictions,

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:34.031
and it's certainly unethical
for a prosecutor to disclose...

00:43:34.114 --> 00:43:35.949
things that happen in a Grand Jury.

00:43:36.033 --> 00:43:38.410
And the Grand Jurors themselves
can't disclose.

00:43:38.493 --> 00:43:40.370
Now, the witnesses, in New York,

00:43:40.453 --> 00:43:43.123
are free to talk about
what they presented to the Grand Jury.

00:43:44.708 --> 00:43:48.754
[Barbara] What flashed into my mind
just now, was the room we were waiting in.

00:43:48.837 --> 00:43:51.214
The ADA came in,

00:43:51.298 --> 00:43:53.801
and called me in to testify.

00:43:55.343 --> 00:43:57.680
I walk in. I sit down.

00:43:58.681 --> 00:44:00.390
I look around the room.

00:44:00.933 --> 00:44:03.226
I see nobody who looks like me.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:06.897
No person of color.

00:44:13.946 --> 00:44:19.242
They were sitting in an area
like a theater.

00:44:19.868 --> 00:44:21.453
Elevated seats.

00:44:22.955 --> 00:44:25.916
There must have been
twenty-something people there.

00:44:27.751 --> 00:44:29.878
One person was reading a book,

00:44:29.962 --> 00:44:32.506
another lady was reading a magazine,

00:44:33.048 --> 00:44:35.676
there was a conversation going on...

00:44:36.594 --> 00:44:41.098
My feelings were,
when I sat in that chair...

00:44:43.559 --> 00:44:47.771
was, "They don't care
about what I have to say.

00:44:47.855 --> 00:44:49.189
They really don't."

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:51.609
They weren't paying attention.

00:44:52.901 --> 00:44:54.194
They weren't.

00:44:54.820 --> 00:44:55.738
OK?

00:44:56.697 --> 00:44:57.740
And...

00:44:59.742 --> 00:45:03.829
I became very angry with myself,

00:45:04.788 --> 00:45:07.082
because at one point...

00:45:09.126 --> 00:45:11.003
I began to cry.

00:45:12.546 --> 00:45:14.422
And I...

00:45:15.549 --> 00:45:18.301
hated that moment.

00:45:19.678 --> 00:45:20.971
Because...

00:45:24.141 --> 00:45:25.851
I felt that...

00:45:27.728 --> 00:45:30.606
you know, they were going to say,

00:45:31.356 --> 00:45:34.693
"Here is another black woman

00:45:34.777 --> 00:45:38.196
who didn't do her job with her child,

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.449
and now she wants us
to make somebody pay."

00:45:43.368 --> 00:45:44.537
That's how I felt.

00:45:45.954 --> 00:45:48.248
When your father came back,

00:45:48.791 --> 00:45:50.083
he said to me...

00:45:52.836 --> 00:45:55.088
"Don't expect anything,

00:45:55.172 --> 00:45:58.050
because it ain't gonna happen."

00:45:58.133 --> 00:46:01.386
-[Yance] And how did you find out?
-They came here.

00:46:01.469 --> 00:46:02.763
[Yance] Who came here?

00:46:04.515 --> 00:46:05.641
[sighs]

00:46:06.767 --> 00:46:08.435
One ADA,

00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:11.396
and a detective.

00:46:14.567 --> 00:46:16.193
[Yance] And what did they tell you?

00:46:17.778 --> 00:46:19.362
They didn't indict.

00:46:19.446 --> 00:46:21.364
They returned a No True Bill.

00:46:26.579 --> 00:46:27.871
[Yance] That was it?

00:46:29.122 --> 00:46:30.708
"I'm sorry."

00:46:34.461 --> 00:46:35.963
[Yance] What happened after that?

00:46:36.046 --> 00:46:38.591
I opened the door and said,
"Thank you for coming."

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:45.222
And I will be very honest with you.

00:46:45.305 --> 00:46:47.516
When that door closed, I collapsed.

00:46:51.144 --> 00:46:53.606
[soft music]

00:47:19.214 --> 00:47:22.384
[Yance] I haven't ever, not once,

00:47:23.677 --> 00:47:25.763
tried to imagine what he looks like.

00:47:28.098 --> 00:47:29.725
I think he looks like...

00:47:34.187 --> 00:47:36.273
no offense to present company,

00:47:36.899 --> 00:47:38.734
every white man I've ever seen.

00:47:43.405 --> 00:47:46.909
I think he looks like...
the ticket taker...

00:47:47.993 --> 00:47:49.202
on the Long Island Railroad.

00:47:49.286 --> 00:47:51.664
I think he looks like, you know...

00:47:53.999 --> 00:47:57.377
the guy in front of me, buying a beer,

00:47:58.253 --> 00:47:59.505
at the bar.

00:48:02.465 --> 00:48:04.467
I think he looks like, you know...

00:48:07.721 --> 00:48:09.682
the schmuck who took my cab.

00:48:11.183 --> 00:48:13.060
He looks like my physical therapist.

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:19.149
He looks like, you know...

00:48:21.359 --> 00:48:23.696
anybody, anyone, everyone.

00:48:25.113 --> 00:48:26.406
He's everywhere.

00:48:30.118 --> 00:48:31.620
He looks like everywhere.

00:48:40.128 --> 00:48:42.005
It's one of those things
you do in high school.

00:48:42.089 --> 00:48:43.298
Everybody just....

00:48:44.216 --> 00:48:45.050
proves themselves.

00:48:45.133 --> 00:48:48.637
And... Ford proved himself
on a daily basis!

00:48:48.721 --> 00:48:50.055
[Kevin laughs]

00:48:50.138 --> 00:48:53.642
He just took on this personality,
I guess, once he realized his size,

00:48:53.726 --> 00:48:57.354
and that he could...
you know, that he could intimidate people.

00:48:57.938 --> 00:49:01.066
It was actually my birthday,
I think it was my 16th birthday,

00:49:01.149 --> 00:49:06.404
and one of the more menacing kids decided
that day was my turn to get picked on.

00:49:06.488 --> 00:49:10.283
We were in a fight,
and one of his friends had a knife,

00:49:10.367 --> 00:49:15.038
and tried to give the knife
to the person I was engaged with.

00:49:15.914 --> 00:49:18.751
Ford just stopped him, and he goes,

00:49:19.502 --> 00:49:21.253
"I wouldn't do that if I was you."

00:49:21.336 --> 00:49:24.297
He just grabbed the knife
and the kid just kind of looked at him,

00:49:24.381 --> 00:49:27.175
and went back to watching the fight.

00:49:27.259 --> 00:49:29.302
You know, I don't know
if he saved my life.

00:49:29.386 --> 00:49:31.138
It was just, Ford was very protective.

00:49:34.767 --> 00:49:37.102
[Kevin] Ford got his license
in the 11th grade.

00:49:37.686 --> 00:49:41.982
Your family had an extremely large,
green station wagon.

00:49:44.067 --> 00:49:47.696
I think it probably fit, like, 15 people
in it, and we made sure that it did.

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:51.074
And we would travel to different towns,

00:49:51.158 --> 00:49:54.161
looking for trouble and looking for girls.

00:49:54.244 --> 00:49:57.164
Ninety-nine percent trouble
and one percent girls!

00:49:57.247 --> 00:49:58.666
[JVC Force: "Strong Island"]

00:49:58.749 --> 00:50:03.170
Living in CI, who the hell am I?
AJ Rok the juice, I get fly

00:50:03.253 --> 00:50:05.506
Cool with the riffing
Guy, keep a handle

00:50:05.589 --> 00:50:08.341
Cause if you don't
I'll wax you down like a candle

00:50:08.425 --> 00:50:10.636
Kill it or shoot it
Buy it or distribute it

00:50:10.719 --> 00:50:12.680
But either way the two's unconstituted

00:50:12.763 --> 00:50:15.808
Styling and wilding
Constantly smiling

00:50:15.891 --> 00:50:18.644
We'll keep trooping
In a place called Strong Island

00:50:19.477 --> 00:50:22.565
So the first time I met him was in '85,

00:50:22.648 --> 00:50:25.568
our freshman year at Howard University
in Washington DC.

00:50:26.401 --> 00:50:31.031
And Ford was the only one
that had on a shirt and tie,

00:50:31.574 --> 00:50:35.661
slacks, and some...
you know, a pair of dress shoes!

00:50:35.744 --> 00:50:38.246
Everybody else was
in sneakers and shorts.

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.833
And it was strange,

00:50:40.916 --> 00:50:43.961
because the first thought
you would think is,

00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:46.088
"Is he a nerd?"

00:50:46.171 --> 00:50:48.674
You know, was he a square, or...

00:50:48.757 --> 00:50:52.177
But he actually wasn't any of that.

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:53.971
You know, he was just...

00:50:56.807 --> 00:50:59.602
He liked to make
a good impression, you know?

00:51:00.811 --> 00:51:03.021
The second semester
was the hardest for him.

00:51:06.191 --> 00:51:08.861
He didn't love Howard, you know.

00:51:10.237 --> 00:51:14.282
He wanted to be home, you know.
He wanted to be home. Yeah.

00:51:22.207 --> 00:51:25.043
[Yance, reading] "January 30th, 1989.

00:51:26.294 --> 00:51:28.922
I'm trying to keep
all aspects of my life balanced

00:51:29.006 --> 00:51:31.216
and be successful in all of them.

00:51:31.299 --> 00:51:33.761
I cut school today to go get a job.

00:51:33.844 --> 00:51:38.473
I got a Sunday-to-Thursday
three-to-11 shift, driving a cab.

00:51:39.057 --> 00:51:42.477
I need the money for Valentine's Day
and Mom's birthday,

00:51:42.561 --> 00:51:44.813
and my other job doesn't start
until the 15th."

00:51:47.941 --> 00:51:48.942
Untitled.

00:51:50.318 --> 00:51:52.571
"Looking at her is peace itself.

00:51:53.405 --> 00:51:55.658
He is at last where he has longed to be.

00:51:56.199 --> 00:51:58.285
Finally, a loving place in her heart.

00:51:59.828 --> 00:52:01.830
He looks upon her
and now begins to wonder

00:52:01.914 --> 00:52:04.416
if the love of someone so radiant
can be possessed.

00:52:06.418 --> 00:52:08.461
He likes holding her in his arms,

00:52:08.546 --> 00:52:12.465
close to him, breathing together
as if from one heart.

00:52:14.092 --> 00:52:17.888
He loves her gentle kisses under his neck
that make him powerless to stand.

00:52:19.848 --> 00:52:22.601
Does she know that she is
the ghost that haunts his dreams?

00:52:23.602 --> 00:52:26.063
Dream, and dream.

00:52:27.064 --> 00:52:28.691
He begins to wonder.

00:52:29.274 --> 00:52:32.235
He looks at her
because he doesn't want to lose

00:52:32.319 --> 00:52:34.613
what he is not certain he truly has.

00:52:35.906 --> 00:52:37.199
He will always look.

00:52:38.951 --> 00:52:40.202
Anything she wants.

00:52:41.078 --> 00:52:42.580
William Ford, Jr."

00:52:44.122 --> 00:52:46.709
Some of the stuff,
I couldn't wrap my mind around.

00:52:46.792 --> 00:52:49.878
I said, "Bread, man,
what are you writing about, man?"

00:52:50.796 --> 00:52:53.256
But in retrospect,

00:52:53.340 --> 00:52:56.259
it could have just been life,
man, you know.

00:52:57.595 --> 00:53:00.097
[Yance] William needed a job
to get him back on his feet,

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:03.684
and my mother helped him get a position
as an assistant teacher.

00:53:04.727 --> 00:53:07.312
He was assigned to the boys' school
on Rikers Island

00:53:07.395 --> 00:53:10.190
and helped teach math
to the young men who were jailed there.

00:53:12.109 --> 00:53:17.740
[Harvey] When Cornbread started teaching
at Rikers, I think his innocence was lost.

00:53:19.199 --> 00:53:21.243
The tragedy...

00:53:24.788 --> 00:53:27.833
to see others...

00:53:30.210 --> 00:53:33.672
that... that look like you, are you...

00:53:36.008 --> 00:53:37.384
still in bondage.

00:53:38.511 --> 00:53:42.973
To try to wrap your mind
around what needs to be done,

00:53:43.766 --> 00:53:45.183
at a young age,

00:53:45.267 --> 00:53:47.728
realizing there's no quick fix,

00:53:47.811 --> 00:53:50.606
and this was...
it was like a culture shock.

00:53:53.066 --> 00:53:57.029
I think the... his experience at Rikers

00:53:57.863 --> 00:54:00.365
deepened him, you know?

00:54:03.326 --> 00:54:05.037
It deepened him,

00:54:05.120 --> 00:54:06.914
to the point that...

00:54:10.793 --> 00:54:13.253
he wanted to... to make...

00:54:16.924 --> 00:54:21.053
make his... efforts count for something.

00:54:22.095 --> 00:54:23.263
Yeah.

00:54:30.187 --> 00:54:33.440
[Kevin] William walked towards the garage
and made a left.

00:54:35.067 --> 00:54:37.695
It wasn't maybe two seconds,
three seconds...

00:54:37.778 --> 00:54:39.530
I just heard, like, a pop.

00:54:41.198 --> 00:54:43.325
By the time I got to the garage door,

00:54:43.408 --> 00:54:45.786
William was backpedaling,

00:54:45.869 --> 00:54:49.247
and he just kind of spun,
I mean, with the biggest eyeballs,

00:54:49.331 --> 00:54:52.585
and he just looked at me and he goes,
"Kev, he shot me."

00:54:53.836 --> 00:54:57.506
And... I wasn't strong enough
to hold him up.

00:54:57.590 --> 00:54:59.466
We both kind of fell to the ground.

00:54:59.550 --> 00:55:02.761
You know, once I asked the guy,
he was named Tom,

00:55:02.845 --> 00:55:04.429
"What kind of gun is it?"

00:55:04.513 --> 00:55:07.891
And he goes,
"Oh, it's a twenty-two. It's a rifle."

00:55:07.975 --> 00:55:09.602
So I kind of chuckled and I...

00:55:09.685 --> 00:55:13.021
you know, it's kind of something you use
to hunt rabbits or birds, you know?

00:55:13.564 --> 00:55:16.233
I remember laughing,
and I'm holding Ford, and going,

00:55:16.316 --> 00:55:18.068
"Dude you got shot with a .22!

00:55:18.151 --> 00:55:20.654
This is hilarious!
Are you kidding me? Get up!"

00:55:21.572 --> 00:55:22.948
And,

00:55:23.616 --> 00:55:24.825
Ford wasn't saying nothing.

00:55:24.908 --> 00:55:26.619
I'm like, "Dude, get up."

00:55:26.702 --> 00:55:28.203
And...

00:55:29.830 --> 00:55:32.249
Then I saw some kind of...

00:55:33.125 --> 00:55:36.294
fluid, you know, on the pavement,

00:55:36.378 --> 00:55:38.255
and I'm just, like, "What is that?"

00:55:38.338 --> 00:55:39.381
And...

00:55:40.799 --> 00:55:43.093
you know, it was never...

00:55:43.176 --> 00:55:44.762
I never saw any blood.

00:55:45.303 --> 00:55:48.891
I just remember things happening very fast
at that point.

00:55:48.974 --> 00:55:52.561
The police came
and they're, like, grabbing me,

00:55:52.645 --> 00:55:56.982
and pulling me to get away from Ford,
and I'm like, "You can't leave him there!"

00:55:57.065 --> 00:56:01.194
And they're like, "There's a person
with a gun inside, get behind the car."

00:56:01.278 --> 00:56:04.114
They put me behind the police car
and they left Ford there.

00:56:04.197 --> 00:56:05.949
I'm like, "You can't leave him here."

00:56:06.033 --> 00:56:09.662
And, one police officer was like,
"You better not move."

00:56:10.203 --> 00:56:12.414
And the next thing I know,

00:56:12.497 --> 00:56:16.084
they were walking out
with the guy that, you know, shot him.

00:56:16.627 --> 00:56:21.423
And he never went in handcuffs,
and that was kind of odd to me.

00:56:21.507 --> 00:56:25.594
I don't remember them putting him in a
police car. They put him against the car.

00:56:25.678 --> 00:56:27.971
And there was a conversation that ensued.

00:56:28.055 --> 00:56:32.392
That's the part that was just weird to me.
I kept paying attention to that part.

00:56:33.101 --> 00:56:35.228
At some point, a limousine pulled up,

00:56:35.771 --> 00:56:38.398
and I remember them walking this kid
to the limousine.

00:56:38.481 --> 00:56:43.403
And I just stood there, like,
"This is crazy that he's not in handcuffs,

00:56:43.486 --> 00:56:45.488
that he's going to get into a limo."

00:56:47.449 --> 00:56:49.827
He was in there for some period of time.

00:56:50.911 --> 00:56:54.247
I kept asking, I said,
"Where's the ambulance? What's going on?

00:56:54.331 --> 00:56:57.501
I've got to go see Ford.
I've got to go see my friend."

00:56:57.585 --> 00:57:01.630
And this cop is like, "You're not going
anywhere, you're coming to the precinct.

00:57:01.714 --> 00:57:03.465
You have a lot of questions to answer.

00:57:03.549 --> 00:57:07.511
You have to understand that a man
has been murdered here tonight."

00:57:07.595 --> 00:57:12.265
It's how I found out that Ford
had already, I guess, passed away.

00:57:13.266 --> 00:57:16.854
And I remember standing there,
and I'm like, "What?

00:57:16.937 --> 00:57:19.106
What do you mean? He's dead?"

00:57:20.023 --> 00:57:22.568
From the time that William was shot

00:57:22.651 --> 00:57:24.277
and that limo pulled up,

00:57:24.361 --> 00:57:25.946
the story got made then.

00:57:26.947 --> 00:57:28.115
You know,

00:57:28.198 --> 00:57:31.577
that this person...

00:57:32.452 --> 00:57:35.789
was not going to jail, period.

00:57:36.707 --> 00:57:37.875
You know.

00:57:38.917 --> 00:57:40.544
It just wasn't going to happen.

00:57:49.512 --> 00:57:51.680
[Yance] So after William was killed,
I was...

00:57:52.222 --> 00:57:54.642
I tried to have
eyes in the back of my head.

00:57:57.520 --> 00:57:59.396
Am I saying what I really mean?

00:58:03.400 --> 00:58:06.069
I remember coming here and I remember...

00:58:08.781 --> 00:58:10.616
you know, kind of...

00:58:13.035 --> 00:58:15.704
you know, kind of coming inside
and seeing you guys.

00:58:18.582 --> 00:58:20.668
And your mom was sitting on the couch,

00:58:21.669 --> 00:58:23.712
and I was kind of scared to approach her.

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:27.215
And she was, like...
She's like, "Come here."

00:58:27.299 --> 00:58:30.803
And I kind of walked over
and she said, "Sit on my lap."

00:58:30.886 --> 00:58:34.848
And she said, "Kevin, just two things."

00:58:34.932 --> 00:58:37.434
She goes, "Don't desert me,"

00:58:38.101 --> 00:58:39.102
and she goes,

00:58:39.186 --> 00:58:42.815
"This is what Ford would have wanted.
He would have wanted you to be there."

00:58:47.152 --> 00:58:48.904
That was just...

00:58:53.909 --> 00:58:56.453
It took the air out of me, you know?

00:58:59.122 --> 00:59:02.084
It left me constantly winded, man.

00:59:04.044 --> 00:59:05.337
You know.

00:59:06.922 --> 00:59:08.381
You know...

00:59:13.512 --> 00:59:16.807
I decided not to let anybody see me upset.

00:59:17.558 --> 00:59:20.519
Because then they would want
to talk about it.

00:59:20.603 --> 00:59:24.397
And I don't want to talk about it.
Back then, I didn't want to talk about it.

00:59:25.608 --> 00:59:30.195
So whenever anybody asked me
how I was doing, I said I was doing fine.

00:59:31.697 --> 00:59:34.199
I started spending some time...

00:59:36.034 --> 00:59:40.497
away from home,
because I couldn't be here by myself.

00:59:41.999 --> 00:59:44.668
Or, you know, with Mom and Dad.

00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:48.171
Mom just spontaneously crying, and...

00:59:48.631 --> 00:59:53.135
If I wasn't with my friends after school,

00:59:53.677 --> 00:59:56.263
I was here, either in my room,

00:59:56.847 --> 00:59:58.348
or watching TV.

00:59:59.517 --> 01:00:01.977
And just... just staying quiet.

01:00:07.232 --> 01:00:09.735
[Yance] After William was killed,
I was scared.

01:00:10.944 --> 01:00:14.281
I was scared to leave my home,

01:00:14.364 --> 01:00:15.949
I was scared to stay.

01:00:20.579 --> 01:00:23.040
I was scared that my parents would...

01:00:33.050 --> 01:00:36.762
Your father said to me,

01:00:39.056 --> 01:00:41.725
"Don't do anything to hurt my daughters.

01:00:45.062 --> 01:00:48.691
Don't do anything to hurt my girls.

01:00:49.357 --> 01:00:51.401
These are vicious people.

01:00:52.986 --> 01:00:56.198
Your son was shot down like a dog.

01:00:58.116 --> 01:01:00.452
You're not going to be with them always,

01:01:00.536 --> 01:01:02.204
I'm not with them always.

01:01:02.871 --> 01:01:04.707
The girls are all we have left."

01:01:06.124 --> 01:01:09.795
[soft, sinister music]

01:01:14.174 --> 01:01:18.261
[Barbara] I wanted him to be angry.

01:01:18.345 --> 01:01:21.557
I wanted him to be outraged.

01:01:23.726 --> 01:01:25.603
I wanted him...

01:01:26.645 --> 01:01:28.021
to...

01:01:29.523 --> 01:01:31.609
I wanted him to get a gun...

01:01:34.402 --> 01:01:38.031
to avenge my son's death.

01:01:41.284 --> 01:01:44.913
He became silent.

01:01:55.132 --> 01:01:58.677
We never sat down as a family
and talked about what happened.

01:02:03.766 --> 01:02:05.433
We just kind of...

01:02:09.104 --> 01:02:12.232
went into our own spaces,
went into our own heads.

01:02:19.823 --> 01:02:22.242
[Barbara] The house had a stillness

01:02:23.577 --> 01:02:26.413
unlike anything I've ever felt

01:02:27.540 --> 01:02:28.999
in my life.

01:02:33.504 --> 01:02:35.213
It was like...

01:02:36.674 --> 01:02:38.717
all the sound...

01:02:39.677 --> 01:02:41.053
left the world.

01:02:47.267 --> 01:02:50.145
I thought...

01:02:51.063 --> 01:02:54.274
that I could comfort your father,

01:02:55.859 --> 01:02:58.987
or... and that he would comfort me.

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:04.034
But he turned his back.

01:03:08.371 --> 01:03:11.291
I would move over, over...

01:03:12.125 --> 01:03:15.378
and... he couldn't go any further.

01:03:16.129 --> 01:03:17.840
He couldn't go any further.

01:03:20.217 --> 01:03:23.762
So I got up and I walked around the bed,

01:03:24.304 --> 01:03:26.223
and I got in front of him.

01:03:28.475 --> 01:03:29.935
I just said,

01:03:32.187 --> 01:03:34.439
"It's not your loss.

01:03:35.816 --> 01:03:38.068
It's our loss.

01:03:41.113 --> 01:03:44.533
We, together, created this child.

01:03:45.367 --> 01:03:48.996
God granted him to us for these years.

01:03:52.625 --> 01:03:57.295
You can't grieve an issue
that came from my body,

01:03:57.379 --> 01:03:58.964
and shut me out."

01:04:03.301 --> 01:04:04.845
And we both cried.

01:04:05.387 --> 01:04:08.724
He embraced me, and we both cried.

01:04:08.807 --> 01:04:10.934
And that's how we went to sleep.

01:04:19.109 --> 01:04:22.905
[soft wind blows]

01:04:32.497 --> 01:04:34.542
[chattering crowd]

01:04:36.627 --> 01:04:38.086
Yance, go down.

01:04:38.671 --> 01:04:41.381
Why don't you let us button up your strap?

01:04:42.090 --> 01:04:42.966
No.

01:04:43.050 --> 01:04:46.887
[Yance] My father had the stroke that
paralyzed him on the left side of his body

01:04:46.970 --> 01:04:49.097
the year after my brother was killed.

01:04:52.643 --> 01:04:54.477
Help me stand up.

01:04:54.562 --> 01:04:56.063
Help you stand up?

01:04:57.606 --> 01:04:58.816
Yeah.

01:05:04.487 --> 01:05:06.406
Right. OK.

01:05:07.199 --> 01:05:08.659
[Lauren] Yance, stand next to Dad.

01:05:08.742 --> 01:05:10.410
-Do you want to stand beside me?
-Yeah.

01:05:10.493 --> 01:05:12.495
-Where are you?
-I'm right here.

01:05:13.872 --> 01:05:15.791
That's the good side.

01:05:16.625 --> 01:05:19.044
The right side is the good side.

01:05:19.127 --> 01:05:21.839
-I know.
-Alright. OK.

01:05:21.922 --> 01:05:23.215
[Lauren] Smile, Dad.

01:05:23.298 --> 01:05:24.592
[he chuckles]

01:05:26.093 --> 01:05:27.636
What else?

01:05:28.220 --> 01:05:30.472
[Lauren] OK, this is good. I'm done.

01:05:32.558 --> 01:05:34.518
And... fading to black.

01:05:34.602 --> 01:05:36.436
[woman laughs]

01:05:37.688 --> 01:05:40.691
When I went to college,
I did start doing what I wanted to do.

01:05:41.233 --> 01:05:46.029
I had filled out an application
for the Rochester Police Department...

01:05:47.239 --> 01:05:48.448
you know?

01:05:49.199 --> 01:05:51.952
And I was looking into
the Monroe Sheriff's County...

01:05:53.203 --> 01:05:55.455
the Monroe County Sheriff's Department.

01:05:55.998 --> 01:05:59.084
I wanted to... I was pursuing...

01:05:59.877 --> 01:06:03.380
you know, being a cop
or being an EMT up there,

01:06:03.922 --> 01:06:05.340
when Dad had his stroke,

01:06:05.423 --> 01:06:06.383
and...

01:06:07.510 --> 01:06:09.678
Mom was like,
"We can't afford to keep you up there."

01:06:17.144 --> 01:06:19.938
[Lauren] 7:30 in the morning.
I had woken up late.

01:06:20.022 --> 01:06:22.733
I got dressed, got my stuff together...

01:06:24.818 --> 01:06:27.905
I had to go to class,
because we had a test.

01:06:32.618 --> 01:06:34.912
I go into the city. I do my thing.

01:06:35.287 --> 01:06:36.413
I come back home,

01:06:38.081 --> 01:06:41.251
but the first thing I see
is Dad's wheelchair folded up

01:06:41.835 --> 01:06:45.047
in the corner of the foyer,
and I'm like, "What's going on?"

01:06:47.883 --> 01:06:50.928
Mom found him outside,

01:06:52.095 --> 01:06:54.557
underneath the porch in a nice shady spot,

01:06:54.640 --> 01:06:57.601
where he liked to sit
and watch people go by.

01:06:57.685 --> 01:07:01.063
[gentle ambient music]

01:07:36.014 --> 01:07:40.352
[Barbara] I did William a great disservice

01:07:41.854 --> 01:07:45.440
in raising him the way we did.

01:07:46.692 --> 01:07:48.443
Because,

01:07:48.527 --> 01:07:52.364
we've always tried to teach you guys

01:07:52.447 --> 01:07:56.493
that you see character and not color.

01:08:01.081 --> 01:08:03.751
And many, many times, I wonder...

01:08:06.044 --> 01:08:09.464
how I could be so wrong.

01:08:31.570 --> 01:08:34.447
[Yance] The only nightmare
I ever remember having

01:08:35.323 --> 01:08:39.411
is my mother,
standing at the top of the stairway

01:08:39.995 --> 01:08:41.204
in her nightgown.

01:08:42.205 --> 01:08:44.082
Her hair is on end.

01:08:44.750 --> 01:08:46.376
There's light behind her.

01:08:47.419 --> 01:08:49.129
I can't see her face.

01:08:50.714 --> 01:08:52.215
She says to me,

01:08:53.509 --> 01:08:55.553
This house is made of bone.

01:08:57.805 --> 01:09:00.015
This house is made of bone.

01:09:02.267 --> 01:09:04.853
This house is made of bone.

01:09:12.820 --> 01:09:15.280
[buzzing]

01:09:29.002 --> 01:09:30.713
[Barbara] I failed

01:09:31.296 --> 01:09:34.717
to keep my son alive.

01:09:37.678 --> 01:09:41.014
I failed you and your sister,

01:09:41.682 --> 01:09:45.769
in not pursuing justice.

01:09:49.397 --> 01:09:51.483
How do you know...

01:09:52.025 --> 01:09:55.153
when and what to do differently?

01:09:56.780 --> 01:09:59.241
[Yance] OK, my dear.

01:10:00.117 --> 01:10:01.910
[Barbara] Thank you very much.

01:10:02.494 --> 01:10:04.872
[Yance] Want to go up to the mirror
to take a look?

01:10:13.338 --> 01:10:16.424
My mother always suspected
that there was something else,

01:10:16.967 --> 01:10:19.887
that she didn't know,
or that she hadn't been told,

01:10:19.970 --> 01:10:23.724
that had happened, that had
contributed to my brother's death.

01:10:25.058 --> 01:10:26.351
She had asked me,

01:10:26.894 --> 01:10:29.354
"Is there anything that you know
that I don't know?"

01:10:29.897 --> 01:10:33.984
And I flat out lied to her
on more than one occasion,

01:10:34.067 --> 01:10:34.985
and said, "No.

01:10:35.944 --> 01:10:37.655
You know everything I know."

01:10:39.990 --> 01:10:41.534
[Yance] And that wasn't true.

01:10:45.495 --> 01:10:48.916
I never told my mom about the conversation
that William had with me.

01:10:48.999 --> 01:10:51.126
I never told my mother that he called.

01:10:54.004 --> 01:10:57.675
I went to college 300 miles from home,
in the middle of upstate New York.

01:10:58.842 --> 01:11:01.762
When I got to Hamilton,
I could finally come out.

01:11:02.805 --> 01:11:04.848
I just didn't have to hide anymore.

01:11:05.473 --> 01:11:07.893
And that's the person
that William didn't know.

01:11:11.689 --> 01:11:15.150
When William had a confrontation...

01:11:15.233 --> 01:11:19.112
Not the night that he was shot
but about a month earlier,

01:11:20.531 --> 01:11:22.199
at the garage,

01:11:22.991 --> 01:11:28.163
and threw a vacuum cleaner
and picked up a... a car door...

01:11:30.708 --> 01:11:32.459
threatening to slam it down...

01:11:34.628 --> 01:11:36.004
He called me after that,

01:11:36.547 --> 01:11:40.217
and told me what he had done, and...

01:11:41.468 --> 01:11:43.929
I was proud of him.

01:11:44.680 --> 01:11:48.058
I cheered him on, for being a bad-ass,

01:11:48.141 --> 01:11:50.435
for not taking shit from anybody.

01:11:50.519 --> 01:11:52.187
And he actually called me.

01:11:54.272 --> 01:11:56.358
He could have called his friends.

01:11:57.359 --> 01:11:59.653
He could have chosen not to call anyone.

01:11:59.737 --> 01:12:02.740
But he called me, and told me about it.

01:12:02.823 --> 01:12:04.992
Because he was proud of himself.

01:12:05.826 --> 01:12:08.161
And I think that he wanted me
to be proud of him.

01:12:08.746 --> 01:12:09.997
And I was.

01:12:12.290 --> 01:12:14.251
And I felt a little bit like...

01:12:16.211 --> 01:12:20.090
even though he might not have fully known
who he was talking to...

01:12:22.425 --> 01:12:25.554
it felt like he was talking
to the real me.

01:12:29.057 --> 01:12:31.143
And that's why that phone call is so dear.

01:12:32.853 --> 01:12:34.563
That's why it's so important.

01:12:36.356 --> 01:12:38.358
And that's why I feel like I fucked it up.

01:12:41.695 --> 01:12:44.031
[Yance] Mark Reilly is accountable
for William's death

01:12:44.114 --> 01:12:45.991
because Mark Reilly shot William.

01:12:47.618 --> 01:12:51.622
But I could have helped William
stay out of that situation to begin with.

01:12:52.831 --> 01:12:56.209
If I had told my parents
about the first incident,

01:12:56.293 --> 01:12:59.630
he would have been stopped in his tracks,

01:12:59.713 --> 01:13:01.715
because they both would have
come down on him.

01:13:02.716 --> 01:13:04.134
But instead,

01:13:04.677 --> 01:13:06.344
I enjoyed...

01:13:07.387 --> 01:13:09.181
my brother, the hero.

01:13:10.849 --> 01:13:14.144
And a month later or so,

01:13:14.227 --> 01:13:15.771
that hero was dead.

01:13:17.731 --> 01:13:20.651
The madness that is my brother's death,

01:13:21.276 --> 01:13:23.236
would drive me mad,

01:13:24.362 --> 01:13:26.406
if I weren't able...

01:13:28.491 --> 01:13:31.662
to hold myself accountable
for at least a small part of it.

01:13:32.788 --> 01:13:35.874
Because then, it sort of...
it grounds it somewhere.

01:13:35.958 --> 01:13:37.626
It puts it on the earth,

01:13:38.669 --> 01:13:42.923
as opposed to in the ether,
or as opposed to... in the unknown,

01:13:43.006 --> 01:13:44.800
or in the anonymous.

01:13:46.259 --> 01:13:50.180
If I don't ground it,
in some way, in myself,

01:13:50.723 --> 01:13:53.016
then it's everywhere, all the time.

01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:54.560
It's ubiquitous.

01:13:57.938 --> 01:14:02.025
And that actually is a greater,
more damaging,

01:14:02.568 --> 01:14:04.695
heavier burden to live with,

01:14:05.320 --> 01:14:09.950
than to blame myself
for not being a smarter 19-year-old,

01:14:10.033 --> 01:14:12.452
when my brother called me and told me

01:14:13.078 --> 01:14:15.497
about this stupid fight that he had.

01:14:20.878 --> 01:14:22.379
Does that make sense?

01:14:29.136 --> 01:14:31.346
[train horn blaring]

01:14:50.032 --> 01:14:52.826
[car passes outside]

01:14:55.746 --> 01:14:58.874
[phone dialing]

01:15:00.626 --> 01:15:03.420
[ringing]

01:15:08.383 --> 01:15:10.052
[man] Detective's Association.

01:15:10.135 --> 01:15:13.639
Good morning. I was hoping to speak
to Detective James Hughes, please.

01:15:13.722 --> 01:15:15.599
This is Detective James Hughes.

01:15:15.683 --> 01:15:18.686
Good morning, Detective Hughes.
My name is Yance Ford.

01:15:19.227 --> 01:15:21.479
I don't know if my name
is familiar to you at all.

01:15:21.564 --> 01:15:25.609
It's familiar because I just got back from
vacation and listened to your message.

01:15:25.693 --> 01:15:29.905
I do remember the case. I remembered it
as soon as I heard your brother's name.

01:15:30.447 --> 01:15:34.284
You know, obviously, the Grand Jury,
when presented with the evidence,

01:15:34.367 --> 01:15:36.411
came back with a No True Bill on the case.

01:15:36.494 --> 01:15:38.288
You know, there was a...

01:15:40.498 --> 01:15:45.754
The Grand Jury pretty much looked
at the case as... a self-defense case.

01:15:45.838 --> 01:15:48.799
They felt it was a justifiable...
shooting.

01:15:49.758 --> 01:15:53.386
The way the Grand Jury went
was supported by the facts and evidence.

01:15:54.096 --> 01:15:58.601
Just, you know, I mean... and that's
part of what made the case so hard.

01:15:59.977 --> 01:16:02.020
But, I mean, this wasn't a thing

01:16:02.104 --> 01:16:04.481
where I thought the Grand Jury
went the wrong way.

01:16:04.565 --> 01:16:07.776
Because I had a number
of different statements from people,

01:16:08.485 --> 01:16:10.571
about incidents prior to this incident.

01:16:11.238 --> 01:16:12.239
And...

01:16:12.322 --> 01:16:15.701
[Yance] I mean, I know that
there was one incident before...

01:16:15.784 --> 01:16:17.327
the night that he was killed,

01:16:17.410 --> 01:16:19.496
because he told me about it.

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:23.458
You know, he'd picked up a car door.
He'd thrown a vacuum cleaner.

01:16:23.542 --> 01:16:24.502
[James] Oh, yeah.

01:16:24.585 --> 01:16:27.379
So I know that that incident occurred.
I don't know...

01:16:27.462 --> 01:16:30.007
Let me see if I can get a hold
of any of my reports.

01:16:30.090 --> 01:16:33.636
OK. Anything... Anything you could
share with me would be really helpful.

01:16:33.719 --> 01:16:36.304
-I can't promise. But if I can...
-No, of course not.

01:16:36.388 --> 01:16:38.557
I really appreciate it.
Thank you very much.

01:16:38.641 --> 01:16:42.144
-I'll talk to you within the next week.
-OK, sounds good. Thank you.

01:16:44.396 --> 01:16:45.898
[exhales]

01:17:00.954 --> 01:17:03.957
[dialing on phone]

01:17:09.922 --> 01:17:12.465
[ringing tone]

01:17:19.472 --> 01:17:21.433
-[woman] Hey, there.
-[Yance] Hey.

01:17:21.517 --> 01:17:22.643
[woman] How's it going?

01:17:23.018 --> 01:17:25.813
I just talked to Detective Hughes.

01:17:26.438 --> 01:17:28.065
Oh, my goodness.

01:17:28.774 --> 01:17:31.694
I need to talk. I just need to hear
your voice for a few minutes.

01:17:31.777 --> 01:17:33.821
I'm right here. I'm right here.

01:17:33.904 --> 01:17:36.073
The fucking vacuum cleaner...

01:17:36.824 --> 01:17:37.908
incident...

01:17:37.991 --> 01:17:39.409
Oh, my God.

01:17:40.202 --> 01:17:42.370
...is why they didn't indict him.

01:17:44.540 --> 01:17:45.749
[woman gasps] What?

01:17:47.543 --> 01:17:51.046
Because he threw a vacuum cleaner.
Because he picked up a car door.

01:17:51.129 --> 01:17:55.634
And because apparently that's enough
to justify reasonable fear.

01:17:55.718 --> 01:17:57.761
What kind of investigation was this?

01:18:00.556 --> 01:18:04.267
The fucking vacuum cleaner.
Oh, my gosh, my love.

01:18:04.935 --> 01:18:06.311
OK.

01:18:06.394 --> 01:18:08.230
Wait. What else did he say?

01:18:08.313 --> 01:18:10.649
Nothing. I just hung up
the phone with him.

01:18:10.733 --> 01:18:12.442
He's going to call me back next week.

01:18:12.526 --> 01:18:14.862
-He's gonna call you back next week. OK.
-Yeah.

01:18:14.945 --> 01:18:16.989
Did you start crying on the phone
or after?

01:18:17.615 --> 01:18:20.158
After. I managed to keep myself together!

01:18:21.243 --> 01:18:23.537
Deep breath. Take a deep breath.

01:18:24.079 --> 01:18:25.080
Thank you.

01:18:25.163 --> 01:18:28.041
-I'll be home soon, OK?
-OK. Talk to you soon. Bye.

01:18:31.545 --> 01:18:33.255
[inhales deep breath]

01:18:40.095 --> 01:18:41.805
[Yance sobs quietly]

01:18:42.556 --> 01:18:44.808
[high-pitched whining noise]

01:18:51.857 --> 01:18:55.152
[screams]

01:19:01.491 --> 01:19:04.494
[breathes deeply]

01:19:13.796 --> 01:19:17.550
[high-pitched whining noise]

01:19:24.682 --> 01:19:27.976
[noise fades to crickets chirping]

01:19:29.562 --> 01:19:32.606
You know, I actually had hoped
to not be in New York that weekend.

01:19:32.690 --> 01:19:36.193
I had hoped to be in Chicago
with a bunch of my friends.

01:19:36.276 --> 01:19:39.613
But I couldn't get anyone to cover
my shifts at the DA's office.

01:19:40.322 --> 01:19:43.992
I didn't have any cash
so I went to the local ATM,

01:19:44.952 --> 01:19:46.954
and as I was doing the transaction,

01:19:47.037 --> 01:19:49.081
I felt something at the back of my head.

01:19:49.164 --> 01:19:52.167
And at first I actually thought it was
one of my roommates,

01:19:52.250 --> 01:19:54.962
maybe playing a joke
or something like that.

01:19:55.045 --> 01:19:57.214
I turned around and there was, you know,

01:19:57.297 --> 01:19:59.466
a man standing there
with a gun in his hand.

01:20:00.968 --> 01:20:02.595
You know, very surreal.

01:20:02.678 --> 01:20:06.599
You know, it's sort of...
people describe... sort of slow motion.

01:20:06.682 --> 01:20:09.643
And that's sort of what I, you know,
went through at that point.

01:20:09.727 --> 01:20:12.855
My, sort of, heart,
at that point was in my throat.

01:20:12.938 --> 01:20:16.942
You know, I could feel my sort of...
constriction, in my throat.

01:20:17.025 --> 01:20:19.695
And I basically made a break for the door,

01:20:20.487 --> 01:20:24.116
you know, giving him the money,
and then there was an explosion.

01:20:25.408 --> 01:20:29.037
And, you know,
I remember actually more the sound first,

01:20:29.121 --> 01:20:31.540
and then this feeling,

01:20:31.624 --> 01:20:35.961
like someone had just, you know...
punched me in the kidney, right?

01:20:36.044 --> 01:20:37.045
And...

01:20:37.796 --> 01:20:39.632
And it was this huge disconnect.

01:20:39.715 --> 01:20:42.342
It just... the explosion was one thing,

01:20:42.425 --> 01:20:46.889
And then there was this feeling in my...
like a really hard punch to the kidney.

01:20:48.140 --> 01:20:50.267
And then it was suddenly this realization.

01:20:50.350 --> 01:20:52.269
"Oh, my God, I've been... He shot me."

01:20:52.352 --> 01:20:55.063
And I remember yelling out, like,
"Oh my God, he shot me."

01:20:56.314 --> 01:20:57.775
And then he was gone.

01:20:57.858 --> 01:21:00.653
I remember thinking,
"I'm not going to die here. Not today.

01:21:00.736 --> 01:21:02.780
Not here on the streets of Brooklyn."

01:21:02.863 --> 01:21:04.489
And... sorry.

01:21:09.161 --> 01:21:12.665
So I put my hand behind my back,
because I was like, "What is that?"

01:21:12.748 --> 01:21:16.126
Like I was feeling, like... moisture.

01:21:16.877 --> 01:21:20.548
And my hand was soaked in blood.

01:21:22.800 --> 01:21:24.092
I'm dying.

01:21:24.677 --> 01:21:26.219
So I...

01:21:26.762 --> 01:21:28.722
I just need to get to a live person,

01:21:28.806 --> 01:21:31.474
someone I can talk to
who's going to know what to do.

01:21:31.559 --> 01:21:34.144
I knew the one place
you always find people is the subway,

01:21:34.227 --> 01:21:36.522
and also the token clerk, and...

01:21:37.480 --> 01:21:40.859
So I actually had a badge,
as a prosecutor,

01:21:40.943 --> 01:21:44.572
and you know, I pulled out my badge
and I said, "Help me, I've been shot."

01:21:44.655 --> 01:21:48.158
And I sort of put it up
against the window and...

01:21:48.241 --> 01:21:51.829
She was like, "OK, alright," you know,
"Hold on, honey. Hold on, honey."

01:21:51.912 --> 01:21:55.498
And, you know, she quickly made a call
on something and then she came out,

01:21:55.583 --> 01:21:58.085
which, I found out later,
is against all protocol.

01:21:58.168 --> 01:21:59.336
But...

01:22:00.170 --> 01:22:02.047
it was this moment of humanity.

01:22:02.130 --> 01:22:03.298
She...

01:22:06.927 --> 01:22:08.261
She just held me.

01:22:08.345 --> 01:22:12.933
And... she encouraged me to sit down,
and I was afraid to shut my eyes,

01:22:13.016 --> 01:22:15.561
because if I fall asleep,
I'm not going to wake up.

01:22:17.479 --> 01:22:18.772
So...

01:22:19.773 --> 01:22:20.899
Sorry.

01:22:25.571 --> 01:22:28.365
They got me up.
They got me into an ambulance.

01:22:28.448 --> 01:22:32.410
I didn't realize that they shut down
the Brooklyn Bridge, to get me over there.

01:22:32.995 --> 01:22:34.997
The other thing I had a vague memory of,

01:22:35.080 --> 01:22:37.332
when I was still down in the subway...

01:22:37.875 --> 01:22:39.918
These two guys coming up and one saying,

01:22:40.002 --> 01:22:42.462
"Hey, man, what happened?
Where did you go?"

01:22:43.380 --> 01:22:47.551
You know. "We saw you, we saw you."
Or, you know, "We couldn't find you."

01:22:47.635 --> 01:22:50.470
And again, I found out later
from the prosecutor

01:22:50.554 --> 01:22:52.222
that one of them was your brother.

01:22:52.931 --> 01:22:55.142
Or was your brother or his friend.

01:22:56.018 --> 01:22:57.936
They apparently had seen part of it,

01:22:58.020 --> 01:23:01.189
or had seen this...
one of the guys running away.

01:23:01.273 --> 01:23:04.067
And I guess one of them
would chase after the guy,

01:23:04.151 --> 01:23:05.903
and one of them went to try to find me.

01:23:08.781 --> 01:23:11.366
Mr. Ford's testimony was basically,

01:23:11.449 --> 01:23:15.078
the guy was running right down the middle
of the street, and he ran after him.

01:23:15.162 --> 01:23:18.081
The guy with the gun.
And jumped on him, and caught him.

01:23:18.165 --> 01:23:20.918
That's my remembrance
of what had happened.

01:23:21.459 --> 01:23:24.755
And I remember that we made an X
right in the middle of the street,

01:23:24.838 --> 01:23:27.257
on a picture,
as to where that had happened.

01:23:29.718 --> 01:23:31.679
I mean, he just, he reacted,

01:23:31.762 --> 01:23:35.265
and I was trying to remember,
was he in the service or something?

01:23:35.348 --> 01:23:37.560
What gave him the training or ability

01:23:37.643 --> 01:23:42.064
to handle himself
in such a... effective way?

01:23:42.147 --> 01:23:43.691
You know? Fearless.

01:23:45.859 --> 01:23:49.530
It was a... it was a heroic act, I think.

01:23:54.326 --> 01:23:58.413
[Yance, reading] "January 1st, 1992.
It's a new year.

01:23:58.496 --> 01:24:00.791
I'd said I was not going
to make any resolutions,

01:24:00.874 --> 01:24:04.252
but there are some goals
that I would like to get accomplished.

01:24:04.336 --> 01:24:07.756
One. By June 1st, I would like
to be a corrections officer.

01:24:07.840 --> 01:24:10.050
Or I will be back in school.

01:24:10.133 --> 01:24:11.927
These are my only two options.

01:24:12.010 --> 01:24:15.806
Two. This year,
I want to stand and not falter.

01:24:15.889 --> 01:24:19.518
I want to calm some of my fears.
I want to feel peaceful.

01:24:19.602 --> 01:24:22.229
This is the year for me to find me.

01:24:22.312 --> 01:24:23.313
January 2nd.

01:24:23.396 --> 01:24:27.275
It's only the second day of the year
and already I'm feeling lonely.

01:24:27.943 --> 01:24:32.114
I'm tired of every aspect of my life
having an undecided feeling.

01:24:32.740 --> 01:24:33.782
January 7th.

01:24:33.866 --> 01:24:37.494
When I got home, there was a letter
from the Department of Corrections,

01:24:37.578 --> 01:24:40.789
telling me I have 30 days
to come in for my weigh-in.

01:24:41.832 --> 01:24:47.004
I'm on a two-day fast, and will be back
down to 245 by Wednesday.

01:24:47.796 --> 01:24:50.841
I couldn't ask
for a more powerful motivation.

01:24:50.924 --> 01:24:52.843
40,000 dollars a year!

01:24:52.926 --> 01:24:55.554
I've been trying to get this job
for almost two years,

01:24:55.638 --> 01:24:58.766
and now it all comes down
to the next 28 days.

01:24:58.849 --> 01:25:02.144
The only thing that I must remember
is that there can be no failure.

01:25:02.227 --> 01:25:03.854
There is no starting over.

01:25:04.396 --> 01:25:07.149
This is the last chance I'm going to get,
and I must take it.

01:25:08.483 --> 01:25:10.903
January 9th. I'm at 241.

01:25:10.986 --> 01:25:14.364
My next goal is to be at 230 by the 19th.

01:25:14.447 --> 01:25:18.368
The goal after that is 225 by the 25th.

01:25:18.451 --> 01:25:22.581
Then, if I am down to 220 by the 30th,

01:25:22.665 --> 01:25:25.000
I will go in on the 31st.

01:25:25.083 --> 01:25:28.796
And I still have two tricks up my sleeve
that I've been holding in reserve:

01:25:28.879 --> 01:25:31.464
Ex-Lax and Lasix.

01:25:33.551 --> 01:25:34.968
January 28th.

01:25:35.678 --> 01:25:37.930
The only problem is,
I think I'm getting sick.

01:25:38.514 --> 01:25:40.933
I'm feeling weaker
than I have felt the whole time.

01:25:41.474 --> 01:25:42.893
I have to hang on until Friday.

01:25:42.976 --> 01:25:46.146
I don't care if I have to crawl
into the place. I will not give up.

01:25:47.147 --> 01:25:49.983
January 30th. The last day.

01:25:50.067 --> 01:25:52.778
I do know that this
is a chance to start over,

01:25:52.861 --> 01:25:54.697
with a whole new everything.

01:25:55.280 --> 01:25:56.990
It's not just a new job,

01:25:57.616 --> 01:25:58.867
it's a new adventure.

01:25:59.868 --> 01:26:02.287
Pull this off, and then you can...

01:26:03.496 --> 01:26:06.792
actually make
a small chunk of the world yours.

01:26:08.251 --> 01:26:12.339
Pull this off, and you can actually make
a small chunk of the world yours.

01:26:12.422 --> 01:26:13.632
Just do it."

01:26:24.101 --> 01:26:25.435
"Dear Candidate,

01:26:26.019 --> 01:26:29.481
This is in reply to your appeal
to the City Civil Service Commission

01:26:29.565 --> 01:26:34.319
of your disqualification for appointment
to the above position, Correction Officer,

01:26:34.402 --> 01:26:36.530
for medical reasons: weight.

01:26:37.447 --> 01:26:40.200
The City Personnel Director
has granted your appeal,

01:26:40.283 --> 01:26:42.494
and you have been marked: qualified."

01:26:44.329 --> 01:26:46.331
It's May 15th,

01:26:46.414 --> 01:26:49.251
so he has been dead now...

01:26:53.589 --> 01:26:55.382
He's been dead for six weeks.

01:27:00.596 --> 01:27:01.722
But, hey,

01:27:02.264 --> 01:27:03.932
he's medically qualified.

01:27:08.311 --> 01:27:10.188
[phone rings]

01:27:13.817 --> 01:27:14.860
[Barbara] Hello?

01:27:15.402 --> 01:27:16.862
Hi!

01:27:17.863 --> 01:27:20.198
Oh, I'm getting there.

01:27:20.282 --> 01:27:22.701
I had my eyes done.

01:27:23.368 --> 01:27:26.914
I certainly look as if
I fought with Muhammad Ali,

01:27:26.997 --> 01:27:29.875
he beat me up, went and had dinner,

01:27:29.958 --> 01:27:33.211
and came back
and punched me in the face some more!

01:27:34.212 --> 01:27:36.381
[laughs]

01:27:37.257 --> 01:27:39.301
The children are doing well.

01:27:39.384 --> 01:27:41.261
They're not children anymore.

01:27:41.804 --> 01:27:43.639
They tell me what to do.

01:27:44.557 --> 01:27:47.768
But every now and then,
you've got to put them back in place.

01:27:48.351 --> 01:27:52.189
You've got to let them know
that you're still the boss.

01:27:53.065 --> 01:27:55.067
I'm still the boss, Yance.

01:27:55.150 --> 01:27:56.735
[Yance] I always knew that.

01:27:56.819 --> 01:27:58.153
[Barbara laughs]

01:27:58.236 --> 01:27:59.822
[Barbara] Thank you for calling.

01:27:59.905 --> 01:28:04.242
You brought bright rays of sunshine
in my life.

01:28:08.956 --> 01:28:10.082
What are you doing now?

01:28:10.165 --> 01:28:13.711
I want to make sure that there's
ice going down the side of your cheek.

01:28:14.587 --> 01:28:16.213
Like, to this place, here.

01:28:16.296 --> 01:28:17.464
OK.

01:28:17.548 --> 01:28:20.258
And down the side of here, also.

01:28:21.093 --> 01:28:24.304
You know, so that we get
some ice on the corners.

01:28:24.387 --> 01:28:29.226
So why don't you put a piece of tape
from here, back there?

01:28:29.309 --> 01:28:32.020
And if you need to take
the earring out, you can.

01:28:32.521 --> 01:28:35.023
[Hughes]
From what I was able to put together...

01:28:36.233 --> 01:28:39.194
...there was a car accident
on February 16th.

01:28:39.277 --> 01:28:43.115
Apparently, it was with a Super Stang
vehicle, driven by Mark Reilly.

01:28:43.198 --> 01:28:45.408
There was an agreement made
at the scene

01:28:45.492 --> 01:28:47.703
that Super Stang would fix the car.

01:28:47.786 --> 01:28:50.080
Then time started to stretch out.

01:28:50.163 --> 01:28:56.044
By March 19th, Lesline and your mom
went to the shop to see what was going on.

01:28:56.128 --> 01:29:01.509
Mark Reilly may have said something
that got your mom... your mom upset,

01:29:01.592 --> 01:29:03.176
which got William upset.

01:29:03.260 --> 01:29:06.096
And William went to the shop
with Kevin Myers.

01:29:06.179 --> 01:29:08.516
William was speaking to Thomas Datre.

01:29:08.599 --> 01:29:10.976
Coincidentally, he's in the news now.
Have you seen it?

01:29:11.059 --> 01:29:11.894
[Yance] I have.

01:29:11.977 --> 01:29:13.646
[Hughes]
William was talking to Datre.

01:29:13.729 --> 01:29:16.064
He was very angry
because he'd upset his mother.

01:29:16.148 --> 01:29:17.900
He was described as "shaking with anger."

01:29:17.983 --> 01:29:20.318
Wanted to know
who disrespected his mother.

01:29:20.402 --> 01:29:23.656
He picked up a car door and was
about to throw it, from a Corvette.

01:29:23.739 --> 01:29:27.492
The girl who owned the Corvette was there
and stepped in. He put the door down.

01:29:27.576 --> 01:29:30.453
At that time William picked up a vacuum,
put it over his head.

01:29:30.538 --> 01:29:33.206
The water kind of,
like, fell out onto him,

01:29:33.290 --> 01:29:35.834
and he threw the vacuum and broke it.

01:29:35.918 --> 01:29:39.046
He picked up a hammer, and began...
He came at Mark Reilly.

01:29:39.129 --> 01:29:40.714
I guess he held him responsible,

01:29:40.798 --> 01:29:43.967
because he may have been the one
who disrespected your mom.

01:29:44.051 --> 01:29:47.137
He never swung it at him, never hit him,
he never threatened him.

01:29:47.220 --> 01:29:49.347
-But he had the hammer in his hand.
-Right.

01:29:49.431 --> 01:29:50.558
Mark was petrified.

01:29:50.641 --> 01:29:53.769
But other members there,
they actually were kind of, like...

01:29:54.311 --> 01:29:55.145
laughing at him.

01:29:55.228 --> 01:29:59.232
And they were calling him a little girl.
I mean, he was obviously frightened.

01:29:59.316 --> 01:30:01.819
Your brother was a big guy,
and he was intimidated.

01:30:02.528 --> 01:30:04.362
After that, William went home,

01:30:04.446 --> 01:30:06.657
and your mom noticed
that he was soaking wet.

01:30:06.740 --> 01:30:08.992
Asked what happened, and he said

01:30:09.076 --> 01:30:11.620
that he was... he was wet
with sweat from the gym.

01:30:12.287 --> 01:30:14.414
And I followed up on that.
I went to the gym.

01:30:14.497 --> 01:30:16.792
But your brother had not been
at the gym that day.

01:30:17.375 --> 01:30:21.589
I was there,
the night of the... Corvette incident.

01:30:22.548 --> 01:30:26.176
You know, William kept saying,
"What do I have to do?

01:30:26.259 --> 01:30:30.514
Why isn't my car being fixed?
You got time to work on everything else."

01:30:30.598 --> 01:30:33.517
And he's... kind of like this.
And then he saw the Corvette.

01:30:33.601 --> 01:30:37.312
I was like, "No, not the Corvette!"
You know, kind of like that, you know.

01:30:37.395 --> 01:30:38.731
And,

01:30:39.439 --> 01:30:40.983
he never damaged the car.

01:30:41.525 --> 01:30:45.237
And it wasn't a situation.
Because there were guys laughing.

01:30:45.320 --> 01:30:48.991
There were people, like... I don't want
to use the word "egging him on,"

01:30:49.074 --> 01:30:50.283
but clowning with him.

01:30:50.367 --> 01:30:53.996
We were laughing, and clowning, and...

01:30:54.538 --> 01:30:58.125
I'd say there were ten to 15 people there.
Scared of what?

01:30:58.792 --> 01:31:02.630
[Hughes] The vehicle was subsequently
released to your... to your mom, I guess.

01:31:02.713 --> 01:31:06.550
There was something about somebody
from the shop following the car home,

01:31:07.092 --> 01:31:10.638
and as a result of that, on April 7th,

01:31:10.721 --> 01:31:13.682
Kevin Myers, again with William,
returned to Super Stang.

01:31:13.766 --> 01:31:16.143
And William was yelling at Tom Datre,

01:31:16.226 --> 01:31:19.563
and ultimately sees Mark,
and goes after Mark.

01:31:19.647 --> 01:31:23.901
Mark retreated into the paint room,
where he grabbed a rifle.

01:31:24.985 --> 01:31:27.404
Your brother walked into the paint room
and approached,

01:31:27.487 --> 01:31:29.907
made a comment about,
"What are you gonna do with that?".

01:31:29.990 --> 01:31:31.074
He came at him.

01:31:31.158 --> 01:31:33.786
And Mark fired one shot.
Hit him in the chest.

01:31:34.327 --> 01:31:35.495
Right.

01:31:36.371 --> 01:31:39.457
There was no stone left unturned in this,
I can tell you that.

01:31:39.542 --> 01:31:41.960
It's just... an unfortunate thing.

01:31:42.044 --> 01:31:45.047
I mean, I certainly put every effort
into this being, you know,

01:31:45.130 --> 01:31:48.175
into this being covered
100 percent, 110 percent.

01:31:55.057 --> 01:31:57.810
[Yance, reading] "April 1st, 1992.

01:31:58.351 --> 01:32:00.896
Tomorrow, I have to go to court
in downtown Brooklyn,

01:32:00.979 --> 01:32:03.356
for my first day of testimony
in the Breen case.

01:32:04.357 --> 01:32:06.777
Mr. Boyar is expecting me by ten o'clock.

01:32:07.570 --> 01:32:10.656
I need to pick up my suit
from the dry cleaners before they close."

01:32:11.574 --> 01:32:16.161
This is where William was
on April 2nd: Kings County Court.

01:32:16.912 --> 01:32:19.331
This is where William was, April 3rd:

01:32:19.414 --> 01:32:21.750
testifying for the prosecution.

01:32:22.375 --> 01:32:25.045
This is where William was
on Monday, April 6th.

01:32:25.754 --> 01:32:30.425
This is where William was
on Tuesday, April 7th.

01:32:31.093 --> 01:32:34.638
Kings County Court,
testifying for the prosecution.

01:32:36.849 --> 01:32:40.686
[Hughes] The vehicle was subsequently
released to your... to your mom, I guess.

01:32:40.769 --> 01:32:44.314
There was something about somebody
from the shop following the car home,

01:32:44.397 --> 01:32:46.483
and... as a result of that,

01:32:47.025 --> 01:32:50.237
on April 7th,
William returned to Super Stang.

01:32:50.320 --> 01:32:53.365
And I think it was just, you know...

01:32:53.448 --> 01:32:56.535
your brother was caught up
in what had happened to your mom.

01:32:57.786 --> 01:32:59.371
[Kevin] I get out. Ford gets out.

01:32:59.454 --> 01:33:03.000
I remember him going into the yard
and somebody coming out of the shop door,

01:33:03.083 --> 01:33:04.668
and meeting him in the yard.

01:33:04.752 --> 01:33:07.588
Words started getting exchanged,
and I said, "Oh, here we go."

01:33:08.130 --> 01:33:11.675
William was arguing outside, in the yard,

01:33:11.759 --> 01:33:14.637
with someone other than the kid
who shot him at the time?

01:33:14.720 --> 01:33:16.179
-Correct.
-[Yance] OK.

01:33:16.263 --> 01:33:20.392
So, the person he was arguing with
was Tom Datre, Jr.

01:33:20.475 --> 01:33:22.853
-He was sort of the owner of the shop?
-Right.

01:33:23.436 --> 01:33:26.857
And that the person who was
in the back, with the rifle...

01:33:27.440 --> 01:33:29.192
Excuse me. His name was Mark Reilly.

01:33:29.276 --> 01:33:31.028
He was an employee of the shop.

01:33:31.654 --> 01:33:32.487
Right.

01:33:33.280 --> 01:33:37.785
I read that Datre Jr. had been,
two years before that...

01:33:38.702 --> 01:33:41.371
arrested for, essentially,

01:33:41.454 --> 01:33:44.667
running a chop-shop out of the garage
at his parents' house.

01:33:45.417 --> 01:33:47.503
He recruited kids,

01:33:47.586 --> 01:33:50.548
and paid them to steal cars.

01:33:50.631 --> 01:33:54.467
And then he chopped up the cars,
and sold... sold off the pieces.

01:33:56.970 --> 01:33:58.806
What I'm trying to figure out is...

01:34:00.599 --> 01:34:03.101
what happened, if anything?

01:34:03.185 --> 01:34:08.481
What did William say,
if anything, to Tom Datre Jr.,

01:34:08.566 --> 01:34:11.902
when they were in the yard,
outside of the garage?

01:34:12.945 --> 01:34:15.197
[Kevin] You know,
he didn't make any threats.

01:34:15.280 --> 01:34:17.365
He didn't say, "I'm gonna beat you up."

01:34:17.449 --> 01:34:20.703
He says, "I got accepted to Police Academy
and Corrections Academy.

01:34:20.786 --> 01:34:23.455
When I become either one,
I am telling you,

01:34:23.539 --> 01:34:25.999
I'm going to get this place shut down."

01:34:27.876 --> 01:34:30.128
Ford was serious about what he was saying,

01:34:30.212 --> 01:34:33.799
but he wasn't doing anything...
anything that night,

01:34:34.633 --> 01:34:38.303
to provoke a fight, or to get shot.

01:34:38.386 --> 01:34:42.224
It was a conversation.
"I'm gonna do this, once I become a cop."

01:34:42.307 --> 01:34:46.394
Then we started to walk away.
That's when Mark walked out.

01:34:46.937 --> 01:34:49.982
He didn't look surprised.
He didn't look panicked.

01:34:50.608 --> 01:34:53.861
He just glanced and made a U-turn,
and went back into the garage.

01:34:54.653 --> 01:34:57.489
That's when William looked at me
and he just kind of went,

01:34:58.031 --> 01:35:00.117
"That's the guy
that cursed out my mother."

01:35:00.200 --> 01:35:01.201
I was like...

01:35:02.327 --> 01:35:05.956
William turned. He didn't say anything.
He just turned and he walked.

01:35:06.039 --> 01:35:08.501
And he got to the doorway. He made a left.

01:35:09.126 --> 01:35:11.044
So then, we heard a pop.

01:35:12.295 --> 01:35:17.551
There was no:
"Stop! I'll shoot you! I'm scared!"

01:35:18.093 --> 01:35:19.845
No scuffle, no fight.

01:35:19.928 --> 01:35:22.139
William turned and was shot.

01:35:26.476 --> 01:35:29.897
I don't understand the self-defense,
because there were options.

01:35:29.980 --> 01:35:33.233
There had to be a back door to the place.
He could have went that way.

01:35:33.776 --> 01:35:36.278
I'm sure some door had a lock behind it.

01:35:36.361 --> 01:35:38.739
Even when they say,
"This happened a month ago,"

01:35:38.822 --> 01:35:42.660
or "He was afraid," or this and that...
OK, so a month ago, you were afraid.

01:35:43.201 --> 01:35:45.245
If that even was the truth that night.

01:35:46.914 --> 01:35:48.791
And after leaving...

01:35:50.458 --> 01:35:52.002
the Grand Jury,

01:35:53.754 --> 01:35:57.174
I kind of got in the car,
and I kind of knew... what they were at.

01:35:57.257 --> 01:35:59.217
I didn't know if it would work,

01:35:59.301 --> 01:36:02.262
but I strongly felt

01:36:02.345 --> 01:36:07.267
that everything was being made
to present a case of...

01:36:07.851 --> 01:36:11.271
"Mark was scared.
He did what he did because he was scared."

01:36:12.940 --> 01:36:15.442
[Yance, reading]
"Gunshot wound of the right chest.

01:36:15.526 --> 01:36:18.987
Hemorrhagic wound track,
perforating right chest wall,

01:36:19.071 --> 01:36:24.034
mediastinum, heart, left lung,
and left chest wall.

01:36:24.660 --> 01:36:28.914
Wound path: front to back,
right to left and downward.

01:36:29.497 --> 01:36:32.960
Deformed, non-jacketed,
small caliber bullet recovered.

01:36:33.502 --> 01:36:36.714
There is no evidence
of gunpowder stippling or soot deposition

01:36:36.797 --> 01:36:38.549
on the skin around the wound."

01:36:39.424 --> 01:36:42.052
There is no evidence
of close range firing.

01:36:43.637 --> 01:36:47.307
How do you measure
the distance of reasonable fear?

01:36:52.813 --> 01:36:54.857
[Yance] What are the contours of fear?

01:36:58.485 --> 01:37:00.195
What do your eyes tell you?

01:37:00.821 --> 01:37:04.241
[soft, eerie music]

01:37:08.245 --> 01:37:10.038
[Yance] Do you see my brother?

01:37:16.587 --> 01:37:19.840
[music swells]

01:37:19.923 --> 01:37:22.425
[Yance] Dredge the river
and you will find him.

01:37:24.845 --> 01:37:27.180
Or someone who could have been him.

01:37:34.855 --> 01:37:36.899
So you tell me,

01:37:37.440 --> 01:37:40.110
whose fear is reasonable?

01:37:44.823 --> 01:37:47.910
[music fades]

01:37:50.287 --> 01:37:51.789
[Yance, reading] "The body is cold,

01:37:51.872 --> 01:37:55.501
and rigor is present and fixed
to an equal degree in all extremities.

01:37:56.376 --> 01:37:58.754
There is an earring hole
in the left earlobe.

01:37:59.462 --> 01:38:02.257
No tattoos or needle tracks are observed.

01:38:02.800 --> 01:38:05.385
The irides are brown,
the cornea are cloudy,

01:38:05.468 --> 01:38:07.721
the conjunctivae are unremarkable.

01:38:08.681 --> 01:38:10.599
The liver is unremarkable.

01:38:11.559 --> 01:38:13.644
The kidneys are unremarkable.

01:38:15.103 --> 01:38:16.479
The heart...

01:38:16.564 --> 01:38:17.981
is unremarkable."

01:38:25.656 --> 01:38:31.369
I looked at... the Grand Jury.

01:38:32.204 --> 01:38:34.414
I looked around.

01:38:34.497 --> 01:38:37.250
I saw who was paying attention.

01:38:38.043 --> 01:38:38.919
OK?

01:38:39.002 --> 01:38:43.006
How could you come to a viable decision,

01:38:43.090 --> 01:38:45.217
if you're reading a magazine,

01:38:45.300 --> 01:38:47.469
if you're doing a crossword puzzle,

01:38:47.553 --> 01:38:49.680
if you're talking with somebody?

01:38:50.222 --> 01:38:53.100
I think serving on a jury,

01:38:53.183 --> 01:38:55.644
and particularly a Grand Jury,

01:38:55.728 --> 01:38:59.565
is one of the greatest... privileges

01:38:59.648 --> 01:39:02.526
of citizenship in this country.

01:39:03.611 --> 01:39:07.155
And you shouldn't take it that way,

01:39:07.698 --> 01:39:10.868
based on race, creed or color.

01:39:11.409 --> 01:39:16.164
You should judge the content
of the evidence presented.

01:39:16.874 --> 01:39:21.003
My feelings were
that they just didn't give a damn.

01:39:21.086 --> 01:39:25.549
And if I die today,
tomorrow or the next day,

01:39:25.633 --> 01:39:29.094
I will die believing
that they didn't care,

01:39:29.177 --> 01:39:33.306
because my son was a young man of color.

01:39:33.390 --> 01:39:36.769
I will always believe that. Always.

01:39:36.852 --> 01:39:38.521
Until the day I die.

01:39:42.691 --> 01:39:44.401
[bird chirps]

01:39:49.948 --> 01:39:54.119
[Yance] It was election day and...
four o'clock in the morning,

01:39:54.870 --> 01:39:56.371
the phone rings.

01:39:56.454 --> 01:40:00.959
I knew because of the time
that something was wrong.

01:40:05.422 --> 01:40:09.134
[breathing through tube]

01:40:12.846 --> 01:40:15.015
[machine beeping]

01:40:23.732 --> 01:40:27.152
[breathing continues]

01:40:43.210 --> 01:40:46.797
[Yance] And there was this... one day,

01:40:47.339 --> 01:40:49.424
before she was in a coma,

01:40:51.426 --> 01:40:55.305
where I thought that she was trying
to pull herself up in bed.

01:40:56.557 --> 01:40:59.059
And I had given her my right hand,

01:41:00.060 --> 01:41:01.770
to, you know...

01:41:02.813 --> 01:41:06.817
I had offered my right hand,
for her to grasp with her right hand.

01:41:07.526 --> 01:41:10.445
And I thought she was trying
to pull herself up.

01:41:11.196 --> 01:41:13.616
And really she was trying
to pull me toward her.

01:41:14.783 --> 01:41:16.118
And...

01:41:16.994 --> 01:41:19.705
it took me a good five minutes
to realize that...

01:41:20.372 --> 01:41:23.375
I was trying to make my mother
more comfortable in bed,

01:41:23.458 --> 01:41:25.961
and what she wanted was for me to hug her.

01:41:40.433 --> 01:41:44.730
[soft, eerie music]

01:42:07.961 --> 01:42:11.131
[music swells]

01:42:22.768 --> 01:42:26.564
[music builds further]

01:42:38.366 --> 01:42:42.245
[Yance] You stumble out of the garage
and into the yard,

01:42:42.788 --> 01:42:44.081
where you fall.

01:42:47.876 --> 01:42:51.004
You lie on the ground, hole in your chest,

01:42:51.547 --> 01:42:53.006
another in your lung.

01:42:56.552 --> 01:43:00.556
You feel the pain and know
you will never see your sisters again.

01:43:01.098 --> 01:43:03.642
Your mother. Your father.

01:43:05.769 --> 01:43:08.188
You wonder how
your family will survive this.

01:43:09.607 --> 01:43:12.484
You realize
that you will not survive this.

01:43:14.486 --> 01:43:19.199
You realize you starved yourself
for 32 days, for nothing.

01:43:21.326 --> 01:43:24.580
You lie on the ground,
bullet through your heart.

01:43:25.623 --> 01:43:28.959
You can't speak, and no one speaks to you.

01:43:30.711 --> 01:43:35.048
You do not know your death may, in fact,
be the actual death of our family.

01:43:36.466 --> 01:43:39.678
You do not know
that we are silent in our grief,

01:43:39.762 --> 01:43:41.388
even with one another.

01:43:43.181 --> 01:43:45.934
You do not know your killer will say
he had to shoot you.

01:43:47.770 --> 01:43:51.189
You do not know your killer
will make you out to be a monster.

01:43:52.566 --> 01:43:54.777
Mark Reilly will make you out
to be a monster,

01:43:54.860 --> 01:43:56.444
and people will believe him.

01:43:58.238 --> 01:44:00.658
You do not realize
that there will be no trial.

01:44:02.159 --> 01:44:05.162
You don't know
that 23 white people will decide

01:44:05.245 --> 01:44:07.623
no crime has even been committed.

01:44:09.542 --> 01:44:12.127
You realize there may be no Heaven.

01:44:14.046 --> 01:44:15.756
There may only be the ground.

01:44:23.471 --> 01:44:26.559
[distant city noise]

01:45:07.432 --> 01:45:10.060
[Ray Charles: "Let the Good Times Roll"]

01:45:10.853 --> 01:45:12.395
Let's have some fun

01:45:13.146 --> 01:45:16.942
You only live but once
And when you're dead, you're done

01:45:17.025 --> 01:45:19.277
So let the good times roll

01:45:21.321 --> 01:45:23.824
I said, let the good times roll

01:45:26.451 --> 01:45:29.037
I don't care if you're young or old

01:45:29.121 --> 01:45:32.457
You ought to get together
Let the good times roll

01:45:36.003 --> 01:45:39.798
Don't sit there mumbling
Talking trash

01:45:40.758 --> 01:45:44.386
If you want to have a ball
You've got to go out and spend some cash

01:45:44.469 --> 01:45:47.430
And let the good times roll, now

01:45:48.724 --> 01:45:50.768
I'm talking about the good times

01:45:53.436 --> 01:45:56.273
Well, it makes no difference
Whether you're young or old

01:45:56.356 --> 01:46:00.068
All you've got to do is get together
Let the good times roll

01:46:03.238 --> 01:46:06.909
[brass solo]

01:46:31.058 --> 01:46:34.937
Hey, you all, tell everybody
Ray Charles is in town

01:46:35.896 --> 01:46:39.817
I got a dollar and a quarter
And I'm just ringing the clock

01:46:40.358 --> 01:46:44.572
But don't let no female play me cheap

01:46:44.655 --> 01:46:49.535
I've got 50 cents more
Than I'm gonna keep

01:46:49.618 --> 01:46:51.954
So let the good times roll

01:46:54.122 --> 01:46:57.835
I tell you all I'm gonna let
The good times roll now

01:46:59.086 --> 01:47:02.089
Well, it don't make no difference
If you're young or old

01:47:02.172 --> 01:47:05.676
All you've got to do is get together
Let the good times roll

01:47:08.428 --> 01:47:12.975
Yeah, no matter whether
Rainy weather

01:47:13.517 --> 01:47:17.354
If you want to have a ball
You got to get yourself together

01:47:17.437 --> 01:47:20.733
Oh, get yourself under control

01:47:22.275 --> 01:47:24.778
Let the good times roll

